TP.register({id:"lp7-g8",lp:7,grade:"8",mode:"curriculum",checks:6,nextGrade:"high school",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY_TITLE="Hours";const STORY=`<p>The Westmill Time Bank keeps its accounts in a green hardback ledger that lives in a drawer at the library, because Olu Brandt does not believe in the spreadsheet and has outlasted two people who did.</p>
<p>She is my grandmother. She has 212 hours in the book and she has never spent one.</p>
<p>I found this out in October, when I was put on the ledger as the person who copies out the monthly balances, which is a job nobody wanted and which took me four Saturdays to stop resenting.</p>
<p>The way it works is that an hour is an hour. You drive somebody to Harrow, you earn an hour. You sit with somebody's dog for an hour while they are at an appointment, you earn an hour. An hour of accountancy and an hour of weeding are the same hour, which is the whole point and which people argue about at the November meeting every single year.</p>
<p>Two hundred and twelve hours is four and a half weeks of somebody's working life.</p>
<p>“You could have the gutters done,” I said. I was copying out the October column. “Twice. You could have the gutters done twice and still have two hundred hours.”</p>
<p>“I could.”</p>
<p>“Your knee.”</p>
<p>“My knee is my own business.”</p>
<p>I let it go for three weeks, which for me is a long time, and then I did not let it go.</p>
<p>“If nobody spends,” I said, “then it is not a bank. It is a list of people being good at each other.”</p>
<p>She looked up at that. She is eighty-three and she gives you her whole face when she is listening, which is a thing you do not notice until somebody does it.</p>
<p>“Say it again.”</p>
<p>I said it again.</p>
<p>“Who is at the bottom of the page?” she said.</p>
<p>I looked. The October column ran from her 212 down to the last line. “D. Carrow. Minus eleven.”</p>
<p>“And how does it look to D. Carrow,” my grandmother said, “to be minus eleven on a page where the woman at the top has never asked for anything?”</p>
<p>I did not have an answer that night. I have one now, and it is not a comfortable one. She had spent fifteen years making certain she was never the one who owed anybody, and she had got so good at it that the top of the page had turned into a place nobody could reach. What she was telling me, in the only way she had, was that she already knew.</p>
<p>In February she had the gutters done. She wrote it in herself, in the ledger, in ink: eight hours, Brandt O. to Ferreira J., gutters.</p>
<p>Eleven days later D. Carrow spent four hours on a boiler.</p>
<p>I do not think those two facts are unrelated. I cannot prove it. It is the sort of thing the ledger is not built to show.</p>`;const story=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${STORY_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${STORY}</div>`;const ART_TITLE="Why Helping Spreads";const ARTTEXT=`<p>Helping is contagious, and the effect is measurable, but it is smaller and stranger than the phrase suggests.</p>
<p>The clearest finding is about watching. Across a run of field studies, people who had just seen someone else help a stranger were more likely to help the next stranger themselves, by margins usually between 5 and 15 percentage points. The effect is largest when the first helper looks similar to the watcher and when the second chance to help comes within a few minutes. It fades quickly. By the next day it is usually gone.</p>
<p>A second finding is about receiving. People who have just been helped also help more, which is sometimes called paying it forward. Here the effect lasts longer but is narrower: it tends to be aimed at the same kind of task rather than at helping in general.</p>
<p>What does not show up is the thing most people expect. Being thanked in public does not reliably raise later helping, and in several studies it lowers it, apparently because a public thank-you settles the account and ends the obligation.</p>
<p>The Westmill Time Bank has run for fifteen years and keeps a complete ledger, which makes it an unusually good record of one small system. Members earn one hour of credit for one hour of help, whatever the help is. Hours are also earned for work on the bank's own projects, the library garden and the Saturday repair table, so the balances in the ledger do not add up to zero. In the last financial year the bank recorded 1,840 hours exchanged among 96 active members.</p>
<p>The ledger shows one pattern the research would predict and one it would not. The predicted one: a member's first exchange is followed, more often than chance, by a second within three weeks. The unpredicted one: the members who earn most are not the members who spend most. Eleven members hold more than 100 hours each and spend fewer than 5 hours a year. The ledger has no column for why.</p>`;const artText=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${ART_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${ARTTEXT}</div>`;const PROP_TITLE="Cap the Balances at Forty Hours";const PROP_SRC="Proposal to the Westmill Time Bank, November meeting. From member Ruaridh Vance.";const PROP=`<p>The bank should cap any member's balance at 40 hours. Three reasons follow, and then one more point.</p>
<p>First, hoarding. Eleven members hold more than 100 hours each and spend fewer than 5 hours a year. Together those eleven hold 1,460 hours, against 1,840 hours exchanged in the whole of last year. That is not a bank. That is a cupboard.</p>
<p>Second, the bottom of the page. Six members are in deficit. The gap between the top line of the ledger and the bottom line is now 223 hours. Nobody at the bottom asks for help on a page that looks like that.</p>
<p>Third, what the research says. People who have just been helped go on to help more. Capping balances forces the top of the page to accept help, which seeds more help.</p>
<p>One more point. Three years ago Brayford capped its time bank at 50 hours, and its membership grew 18 percent the following year, which shows that caps bring people in.</p>
<p>Also worth saying: our treasurer has done this job for eleven years without a single error, and I would not want anyone to think this proposal reflects on her.</p>`;const propText=()=>`<article><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${PROP_TITLE}</h3><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 8px">${PROP_SRC}</p><div class="passage sm">${PROP}</div></article>`;const SRC_A_CARD="Report: “Observed Helping After Observed Help: A Replication Across Four Field Sites.” By Dr. Priya Ramaswamy, social psychologist, Lowell Institute, 2024.";const SRC_A_TEXT="Participants who had just watched someone help a stranger went on to help the next stranger 11 percentage points more often than people who had watched nothing. The difference was no longer detectable 24 hours later.";const SRC_B_CARD="A post on a community forum by a user named ledgerhawk. No date.";const SRC_B_TEXT="time banks always collapse because the givers get tired. read that somewhere. the fix is obviously to just pay people.";const SOURCES=()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.55rem">Research question: does watching somebody help make a person more likely to help?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${srcCard("Source A",SRC_A_CARD,SRC_A_TEXT)}${srcCard("Source B",SRC_B_CARD,SRC_B_TEXT)}</div>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:10px 20px;margin:14px 0"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 6px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.22rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const bigTable=(head,rows,cap)=>`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="margin:14px auto;font-size:1.28rem">${cap?`<caption style="font-size:1.1rem;padding-bottom:6px">${cap}</caption>`:""}<tr>${head.map(h=>`<th style="text-align:center">${h}</th>`).join("")}</tr>${rows.map(r=>`<tr>${r.map((c,i)=>i?`<td style="text-align:center">${c}</td>`:`<th>${c}</th>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</table></div>`;function srcCard(tag,head,body){return`<article style="flex:1 1 320px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${tag}</h3><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${body}</p></article>`}const lead=t=>`<p class="big">${t}</p>`;const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/&/g,"&amp;").replace(/</g,"&lt;").replace(/>/g,"&gt;");function plot(o){const m={l:o.yl?76:38,r:26,t:o.title?34:20,b:o.xl?54:30},sx=o.sx,sy=o.sy,W=o.x1*sx,H=o.y1*sy;const X=x=>m.l+x*sx,Y=y=>m.t+(o.y1-y)*sy,gx=o.gx||1,gy=o.gy||1,lx=o.lx||gx,ly=o.ly||gy;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+m.l+m.r} ${H+m.t+m.b}" width="${o.w||W+m.l+m.r}" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">`;if(o.title)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="21" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.title}</text>`;s+=`<rect x="${m.l}" y="${m.t}" width="${W}" height="${H}" fill="var(--surface)"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=gx)s+=`<line x1="${X(x)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(x)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=gy)s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(y)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(y)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(0)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(0)}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/><line x1="${X(0)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(0)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=lx)s+=`<text x="${X(x)}" y="${Y(0)+18}" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${+x.toFixed(3)}</text>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=ly)s+=`<text x="${m.l-7}" y="${Y(y)}" font-size="13" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${+y.toFixed(3)}</text>`;if(o.xl)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="${m.t+H+46}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.xl}</text>`;if(o.yl)s+=`<text transform="translate(20,${m.t+H/2}) rotate(-90)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.yl}</text>`;(o.paths||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<polyline points="${p.pts.map(q=>X(q[0])+","+Y(q[1])).join(" ")}" fill="none" stroke="${p.c||"var(--sea)"}" stroke-width="3.4" stroke-linejoin="round"${p.dash?' stroke-dasharray="9 6"':""}/>`});(o.pts||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<circle cx="${X(p[0])}" cy="${Y(p[1])}" r="6" fill="var(--coral)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.4"/>`});(o.labels||[]).forEach(l=>{s+=`<text x="${X(l[0])}" y="${Y(l[1])}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="${l[2]||"start"}" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="4">${l[3]}</text>`});return s+"</svg>"}const pointGraph=()=>plot({alt:"Graph with five plotted points: 1 comma 3, 3 comma 4, 5 comma 2, 5 comma 8, and 7 comma 6.",title:"Graph D",x1:8,y1:10,sx:38,sy:22,gx:1,lx:1,gy:2,ly:2,xl:"Input",yl:"Output",pts:[[1,3],[3,4],[5,2],[5,8],[7,6]]});const tableA=()=>bigTable(["Month","1","2","3","4"],[["Hours exchanged","120","155","155","180"]],"Table A");const tableB=()=>bigTable(["Member","Brandt","Idris","Carrow","Ferreira"],[["Hours held","212","60","&minus;11","60"]],"Table B");const tableC=()=>bigTable(["Hours of credit spent","4","6","4","9"],[["Jobs it paid for","1","2","3","2"]],"Table C");const pairTable=()=>bigTable(["Members in the group, n","2","3","4","5"],[["Different pairs they make, p","1","3","6","10"]],"Pairs in a group");const T=(x,y,t,a,fs)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${a||"middle"}" font-size="${fs||16}" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="3.5">${t}</text>`;const svg=(w,h,width,alt,inner)=>`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${h}" width="${width}" role="img" aria-label="${alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">${inner}</svg>`;const gridFig=()=>F.coordGrid({max:10,quad4:true,size:420,polys:[[[2,1],[6,1],[6,4]]],points:[[2,1,"P"],[6,1,"Q"],[6,4,"R"]]});const ladderFig=()=>svg(260,230,250,"A vertical wall, a ground line, and a ladder leaning from the ground to the top of the wall. The ladder is labeled 17 feet. The distance along the ground from the wall to the foot of the ladder is labeled 8 feet. There is a right angle mark where the wall meets the ground, and the height the ladder reaches is marked with a question mark.",`<line x1="34" y1="196" x2="238" y2="196" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3"/>
   <line x1="56" y1="196" x2="56" y2="46" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="5"/>
   <line x1="56" y1="46" x2="136" y2="196" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="3.5"/>
   <path d="M68 196 v-12 h-12" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>
   <circle cx="136" cy="196" r="5" fill="var(--coral)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>
   ${T(48,124,"? ft","end")}${T(96,218,"8 ft")}${T(112,106,"17 ft","start")}`);const crateFig=()=>svg(330,215,320,"A closed crate drawn in three dimensions with the long front edge labeled 7 inches, the front upright edge labeled 6 inches, and the depth edge labeled 6 inches, and a dashed line drawn inside it from one bottom front corner to the opposite top back corner.",`<polygon points="56,78 168,78 222,42 110,42" fill="var(--gold)" fill-opacity=".45" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   <polygon points="168,78 222,42 222,138 168,174" fill="var(--sea-soft)" fill-opacity=".7" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   <rect x="56" y="78" width="112" height="96" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   <line x1="56" y1="174" x2="222" y2="42" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-dasharray="7 5"/>
   ${T(112,200,"7 in")}${T(50,130,"6 in","end")}${T(234,96,"6 in","start")}${T(146,122,"?","start",20)}`);const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const REPLY_TITLE="The Ledger Does Not Say That";const REPLY_SRC="Reply to the November proposal. From Marit Haugen, treasurer of the Westmill Time Bank.";const REPLY=`<p>I have no objection to a debate about caps. I do object to the numbers.</p>
<p>Mr Vance says the eleven members hold 1,460 hours. They do not. 1,460 is what those eleven have earned since they joined, which for the longest-standing of them is fifteen years. Their balances as they stand in the ledger today total 1,208 hours, because nine of the eleven have spent something at some point. The 1,460 figure is a column heading away from being right.</p>
<p>On Brayford, the cap was not on every balance. Brayford capped new members at 50 hours for their first year only. Its membership grew the same year the Harrow bus route was extended to Brayford, which the Brayford secretary told me herself.</p>
<p>Where I agree with him is the bottom of the page. Six members are in deficit, and the gap from the top line to the bottom is 223 hours, and that gap is doing something to the people at the bottom of it. I do not think a cap is the way to close it. I do think it has to be closed.</p>`;const replyText=()=>`<article><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${REPLY_TITLE}</h3><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 8px">${REPLY_SRC}</p><div class="passage sm">${REPLY}</div></article>`;const satTable=()=>bigTable(["Saturdays worked, s","2","4","6"],[["Hours earned by P","5","10","15"]],"Member P's hours from the garden project");const satGraph=()=>plot({alt:"A graph of hours earned against Saturdays worked for member P. One straight line rises from 0 comma 0 through 2 comma 5, 4 comma 10 and 6 comma 15.",title:"Member P",x1:8,y1:24,sx:40,sy:10,gx:1,lx:1,gy:1,ly:4,xl:"Saturdays worked",yl:"Hours earned",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[8,20]]}],pts:[[0,0],[2,5],[4,10],[6,15]]});const sysGraph=()=>plot({alt:"A graph of hours earned against sessions worked. A solid line rises from 0 comma 2. A dashed line rises from 0 comma 12. The two lines cross.",title:"Two ways to earn hours",x1:8,y1:28,sx:40,sy:9,gx:1,lx:1,gy:1,ly:4,xl:"Sessions worked",yl:"Hours earned",paths:[{pts:[[0,2],[8,26]]},{pts:[[0,12],[8,20]],c:"var(--coral)",dash:true}],labels:[[6.6,25.5,"end","Garden project"],[1.2,14.8,"start","Repair table"]]});const SCAT=[[20,24],[35,21],[50,20],[60,40],[65,17],[80,15],[95,12],[110,9],[125,7],[140,4]];const scatterFig=()=>plot({alt:"A scatter plot of ten members. Hours earned runs along the bottom from 0 to 150 and hours spent runs up the side from 0 to 45. Nine of the points fall in a line sloping downward from about 20 comma 24 to about 140 comma 4. One point sits well above the others at 60 comma 40.",title:"Ten members: hours earned and hours spent",x1:150,y1:45,sx:3.2,sy:4.6,gx:10,lx:30,gy:5,ly:15,xl:"Hours earned",yl:"Hours spent",pts:SCAT});const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Hours",short:"Hours",parts:[{std:["RL.8.1"],dur:145,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence for what a story says and for inferences drawn from it",html:story,qs:["Why does the Time Bank keep its accounts in a paper ledger? Read me the exact words that prove it.","Which one is the strongest evidence that Olu Brandt already understood the narrator's objection before the narrator made it? Pick A, B, or C and tell me why. A: “She looked up at that.” B: “And how does it look to D. Carrow, to be minus eleven on a page where the woman at the top has never asked for anything?” C: “My knee is my own business.”","The narrator says the answer arrived later and “is not a comfortable one.” What is that answer? Read me the words that most strongly support it.","The last three paragraphs give two dated facts and one claim the narrator cannot prove. Name the two facts, read me the sentence that states the claim, and say why the narrator cannot prove it."],key:["Because Olu Brandt will not use a spreadsheet and has outlasted the people who wanted one. Reads “because Olu Brandt does not believe in the spreadsheet and has outlasted two people who did.” (RL.8.1, explicit evidence)","B. Her question names the exact harm the narrator had just described, and it names it from the other end of the page, which the narrator had not thought of. A shows only that she was listening, and C is a refusal that could mean several things. (RL.8.1, strongest evidence)","That her never spending was not generosity but a way of making sure she never owed anybody, and that it had put the top of the page out of everyone's reach. Strongest support: “She had spent fifteen years making certain she was never the one who owed anybody, and she had got so good at it that the top of the page had turned into a place nobody could reach,” and “What she was telling me, in the only way she had, was that she already knew.” (RL.8.1, inference with the strongest support)","Fact one: in February she had the gutters done and wrote it into the ledger herself, “eight hours, Brandt O. to Ferreira J., gutters.” Fact two: “Eleven days later D. Carrow spent four hours on a boiler.” The claim is “I do not think those two facts are unrelated.” It cannot be proved because the ledger records what was exchanged and not why, which the narrator says outright: “It is the sort of thing the ledger is not built to show.” (RL.8.1, explicit evidence and the limits of it)"]}]},{id:2,title:"One In, One Out",short:"Functions",parts:[{std:["8.F.A.1"],dur:165,dir:"Use the three tables and Graph D. Answer out loud, and use the definition of a function in every answer.",desc:"understanding a function as a rule that assigns exactly one output to each input, and reading the graph of a function",html:()=>lead("A function is a rule that gives each input exactly one output. These are four sets of records the Time Bank keeps."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:4px">${tableA()}${tableB()}${tableC()}${pointGraph()}</div>`,qs:["Is Table A a function? Is Table B a function? Use the definition in each answer.","Is Table C a function? Explain.","Is the set of points in Graph D a function? How can you tell by looking at the graph?","A rule says: to each line in the ledger, assign the member who earned the hours. Is that a function? Now turn it around: to each member, assign a line they earned hours on. Is that a function?","A member starts the year with 212 hours and spends s of them. The rule for the balance is 212 &minus; s. Find the output when s = 8 and write the ordered pair. Then describe what the graph of this function looks like."],key:["Table A is a function. Each input, months 1, 2, 3, and 4, appears once and has exactly one output. Table B is also a function. Each of the four members appears once with one number of hours, and the fact that Idris and Ferreira share the output 60 does not matter, because the definition only forbids one input from having two different outputs. Partial: one of the two tables answered is approaching. (8.F.A.1, the definition of a function)","No. The input 4 appears twice, once with 1 and once with 3, so that input does not get exactly one output. Partial: no with no account of the repeated input is approaching. (8.F.A.1)","No. The input 5 has two points, (5, 2) and (5, 8), so one input has two outputs. By eye, a vertical line drawn at 5 would cross the graph twice, and the graph of a function is crossed at most once by any vertical line. Or: naming the vertical line test counts as the by-eye reason. (8.F.A.1, the graph of a function)","The first rule is a function, because each line in the ledger records exactly one member earning the hours. The second is not, because a member has many lines, so that input would be assigned several outputs. Partial: one of the two rules answered is approaching. (8.F.A.1, deciding whether a rule is a function)","204 hours, because 212 − 8 = 204. The ordered pair is (8, 204). The graph is the set of all ordered pairs (s, 212 − s), and because every hour spent takes the balance down by the same amount, those pairs lie on a straight line that slopes down. Partial: the output and the pair right with nothing said about the graph is approaching. (8.F.A.1, the graph of a function is the set of ordered pairs)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Say It Again",short:"Turning Line",parts:[{std:["RL.8.3"],dur:145,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the line or the sentence you are talking about.",desc:"explaining how lines of dialogue and incidents propel the action, reveal character, and provoke a decision",html:story,qs:["The narrator says one line that changes the whole conversation: “If nobody spends, then it is not a bank. It is a list of people being good at each other.” How does that line change the moment? What does Olu's answer, “Say it again,” reveal about her?","Being given the job of copying out the monthly balances is the incident that starts everything. How does it propel the action?","“Your knee.” “My knee is my own business.” What does that exchange reveal, and why does the author put it before the argument rather than after it?","What decision does the November conversation finally provoke, and where in the story do you see it? What does the way she records it reveal about her?"],key:["It moves the argument off her knee and onto the bank itself, so she can answer it without defending her own comfort. “Say it again” reveals that she is not dismissing it: she stops, asks for it twice, and then answers with a question of her own. It also reveals somebody who has thought about this before, because her reply is ready and goes further than the narrator's point. (RL.8.3, dialogue propels the action and reveals character)","It puts the narrator in front of the numbers every month, with no choice about it, which is how the 212 and the minus eleven end up on the same page in front of the same person. The story says it took “four Saturdays to stop resenting,” so the job is also what turns a chore into attention. Without the incident there is no argument, because nobody else is reading the column. (RL.8.3, an incident propels the action)","It reveals that her refusal has a cost she will not discuss, and that the narrator already knows what the hours could buy. Putting it first makes the later argument necessary: the direct, personal approach has been shut down, so the narrator has to find a reason that is not about Olu at all. It also sets up the end, because when she finally spends, it is on gutters and not on the knee. (RL.8.3, an incident reveals character)","She has the gutters done in February: “She wrote it in herself, in the ledger, in ink: eight hours, Brandt O. to Ferreira J., gutters.” Writing it herself, in ink, in the public book, makes the spending as visible as the hoarding had been, which is the whole point if the problem was how the page looked to everyone else. It is the same person solving it the same way: through the ledger. (RL.8.3, dialogue provokes a decision)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Why Helping Spreads",short:"The Article",parts:[{std:["RI.8.1"],dur:145,dir:"Read the article to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence from an informational text for an explicit answer and for inferences",html:artText,qs:["How many hours did the bank record last year, and among how many members? Read me the exact words.","Which single sentence most strongly supports the claim that the contagion effect is short-lived? Pick A, B, or C and tell me why. A: “Helping is contagious, and the effect is measurable, but it is smaller and stranger than the phrase suggests.” B: “It fades quickly. By the next day it is usually gone.” C: “The effect is largest when the first helper looks similar to the watcher and when the second chance to help comes within a few minutes.”","Claim: thanking people in public is not a reliable way to get more helping. Find the two parts of one sentence that together most strongly support this claim and read them to me.","The article ends, “The ledger has no column for why.” What does that imply about what the Westmill records can and cannot settle? Read me the words that most strongly support your inference.","How does the article tell the watching effect apart from the receiving effect? Read me the exact words for each one."],key:["1,840 hours among 96 active members. Reads “In the last financial year the bank recorded 1,840 hours exchanged among 96 active members.” (RI.8.1, explicit evidence)","B. It states the duration directly and is the only sentence that says when the effect ends. A says the effect is smaller and stranger than expected, which is about size, not time. C names the conditions under which the effect is largest, which is close but still not a claim about how long it lasts. (RI.8.1, strongest evidence)","“Being thanked in public does not reliably raise later helping,” and “in several studies it lowers it.” Both halves are needed: the first says the expected effect is not reliable, and the second says that in some cases it runs the other way. The reason given after them, about settling the account, is an explanation rather than evidence. (RI.8.1, two parts that together support a claim)","It implies that the ledger can show what happened and when, but cannot show why anybody did it, so the patterns in it can be measured but not explained from the ledger alone. Strongest support: “The unpredicted one: the members who earn most are not the members who spend most,” immediately followed by “The ledger has no column for why.” The article states the pattern and then says outright that the record cannot account for it. (RI.8.1, inference with the strongest support)","Watching: “people who had just seen someone else help a stranger were more likely to help the next stranger themselves,” and it “fades quickly.” Receiving: “People who have just been helped also help more,” and that effect “lasts longer but is narrower: it tends to be aimed at the same kind of task rather than at helping in general.” The two are told apart by what triggers them, by how long they last, and by how broad they are. (RI.8.1, explicit evidence for a comparison)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Straight or Not",short:"Linear?",parts:[{std:["8.F.A.3"],dur:170,dir:"Use the table for numbers 3 and 4. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer. Sketch number 2.",desc:"interpreting y = mx + b as a linear function and giving examples of functions that are not linear",html:()=>lead("Credits are worked out one way and the number of possible pairings in a group grows another way. Only one of those is a linear function."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${pairTable()}</div>`,qs:["A driver is credited 0.5 of an hour for turning out plus 1 hour for every hour on the road, so the credit is c = t + 0.5. Is that a linear function? Name m and b and say what each one means here.","Sketch the graph of c = t + 0.5 from t = 0 to t = 4. Name two points that are on your line.","Look at the table. Is the number of pairs a linear function of the number of members? Work out the changes and explain.","Give an example of a function that is not linear, other than the one in the table, and say how you know it is not linear.","Which of these define linear functions? (a) y = 9 &minus; 2x (b) y = x<sup>2</sup> &minus; 1 (c) y = 7 (d) y = 6 ÷ x (e) 4x + 3y = 24","A member owes 24 hours and works off 3 a week, so the hours still owed are r = 24 &minus; 3w. Is it linear? What do the 24 and the &minus;3 mean, and what does the graph look like?"],key:["Yes. It has the form y = mx + b, so its graph is a straight line. m = 1 is the slope, one hour of credit for each hour on the road. b = 0.5 is the value when t = 0, the half hour credited just for turning out. Partial: yes with one of m and b not explained is approaching. (8.F.A.3, interpreting y = mx + b)","A straight line from (0, 0.5) up to (4, 4.5). Two points, such as (0, 0.5) and (4, 4.5), or (1, 1.5), (2, 2.5), (3, 3.5). Partial: a correct line with only one point named is approaching. (8.F.A.3, the graph of a linear function is a straight line)","No, it is not linear. From 2 members to 3 the pairs change by 3 − 1 = 2. From 3 to 4 they change by 6 − 3 = 3. From 4 to 5 they change by 10 − 6 = 4. The number of members goes up by the same step each time but the number of pairs does not change by the same amount, so the rate of change is not constant, the graph curves upward, and the rule cannot be written as y = mx + b. Or: sketch the four pairs and show they curve. Partial: no with one difference worked out is approaching. (8.F.A.3, recognizing a function that is not linear)","Any correct example with a reason, such as: the area of a square notice board, A = s², where s = 1, 2, 3, 4 gives 1, 4, 9, 16 and the differences 3, 5, 7 are not equal, so the graph curves. Also fine: y = 2ˣ, y = 1 ÷ x, or the volume of a cube. The student must say why it is not linear, not just name it. (8.F.A.3, giving examples of functions that are not linear)","Linear: (a), with m = −2 and b = 9; (c), with m = 0 and b = 7, which graphs as a horizontal line; and (e), because solving for y gives y = −4/3 x + 8. Not linear: (b), because x is squared, and (d), because x is in the denominator. Partial: (c) and (e) are the two usually missed, so three or four of the five sorted right is approaching. (8.F.A.3)","Linear. b = 24 is the number of hours still owed at w = 0, and m = −3 means 3 fewer owed each week, so the line falls as w goes up. The graph is a straight line starting at (0, 24) and reaching 0 at w = 8, because 24 ÷ 3 = 8. Partial: linear with one of the two numbers explained is approaching. (8.F.A.3, interpreting m and b in context)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Good At Each Other",short:"Word Choice",parts:[{std:["RL.8.4"],dur:140,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the words you are talking about.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases, including connotation, figurative language, and the effect of specific word choices",html:story,qs:["“It is a list of people being good at each other.” Most people would say being good <em>to</em> each other. What does <em>at</em> do instead, and why is it the right word for what the narrator means?","“She gives you her whole face when she is listening.” What kind of language is that, and what does it tell you about her?","“She wrote it in herself, in the ledger, in ink.” What does <em>in ink</em> suggest here that <em>in pencil</em> would not? Why does the author spend three phrases on it instead of one?","“That is not a bank. That is a cupboard.” Compare what those two words suggest. (The second one is from the proposal, and the narrator's line is “If nobody spends, then it is not a bank.”) Which words in the story carry the same idea?","“I let it go for three weeks, which for me is a long time, and then I did not let it go.” What is the effect of repeating the same phrase in the negative?"],key:["To carries a direction: one person does a good thing to another. At carries aim and effort without a result, the way you throw something at a wall. The narrator means a group of people all producing goodness in one another's direction with nothing arriving: hours earned and nobody receiving them. That is exactly the failure she is describing, so the odd preposition is the argument. (RL.8.4, connotative meaning of a word choice)","It is figurative. Nobody literally hands over a face. It turns listening into something given, which makes attention a thing with a cost, in a story about what people do and do not give each other. It tells you she is fully present and that this is unusual enough to be worth a sentence: “a thing you do not notice until somebody does it.” (RL.8.4, figurative language)","Ink cannot be rubbed out, so the entry is permanent and public, and writing it herself means she did not get somebody else to make the record. Pencil would leave it provisional. The three phrases, herself, in the ledger, in ink, slow the sentence down so that each choice registers separately, which matches a character who has spent fifteen years being careful about exactly this page. (RL.8.4, the effect of a specific word choice)","A bank is a place where things move: you put in and you take out, and it works only if both happen. A cupboard is where things are kept and nothing moves. The same idea runs through the story in “she has never spent one,” in “the top of the page had turned into a place nobody could reach,” and in the narrator's own line about a list rather than a bank. (RL.8.4, connotative meaning)","The repetition marks a decision rather than an event. The first half admits she tried to drop it, and the flat second half, with no new words and no explanation, shows that nothing changed her mind, she simply stopped waiting. Saying it the second time in the negative also makes three weeks sound short, which is the joke in “which for me is a long time.” (RL.8.4, the effect of word choice on meaning and tone)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Move the Plot",short:"Transformations",parts:[{std:["8.G.A.3"],dur:175,dir:"Use the grid. Work on your paper and hold it up. Give every answer as coordinates.",desc:"describing the effect of translations, reflections, rotations, and dilations on two-dimensional figures using coordinates",html:()=>lead("The library garden is planned on a grid. Triangle PQR marks a raised bed, with P at (2, 1), Q at (6, 1), and R at (6, 4)."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${gridFig()}</div>`,qs:["The bed is moved by the rule (x, y) &rarr; (x &minus; 5, y + 3). Give the coordinates of the three image points. What kind of transformation is that?","Reflect triangle PQR over the y-axis. Write the rule in the form (x, y) &rarr; (___, ___) and give the three image points.","Reflect triangle PQR over the x-axis. Give the rule and the three image points.","Rotate triangle PQR 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin. Give the rule and the three image points.","Rotate triangle PQR 180 degrees about the origin. Give the rule and the three image points.","Dilate triangle PQR from the origin by a scale factor of 2. Give the three image points. Which measurements stay the same and which change? Say what happens to each side length and to the area."],key:["P becomes (−3, 4), Q becomes (1, 4), and R becomes (1, 7). It is a translation, 5 to the left and 3 up. Side lengths and angles are unchanged. Or: count the squares on the grid instead of using the rule. Partial: the three points right with the transformation not named is approaching. (8.G.A.3, translation)","(x, y) → (−x, y). P becomes (−2, 1), Q becomes (−6, 1), R becomes (−6, 4). The y-coordinate is kept and the x-coordinate changes sign. Partial: the rule right with a point wrong, or three right points with no rule, is approaching. (8.G.A.3, reflection over the y-axis)","(x, y) → (x, −y). P becomes (2, −1), Q becomes (6, −1), R becomes (6, −4). Partial: the rule right with a point wrong, or three right points with no rule, is approaching. (8.G.A.3, reflection over the x-axis)","(x, y) → (−y, x). P becomes (−1, 2), Q becomes (−1, 6), R becomes (−4, 6). Check one: Q is (6, 1), so the image is (−1, 6). Or: trace the triangle and turn the paper a quarter turn, or call it a 270° clockwise turn. Partial: the rule right with a point wrong is approaching. (8.G.A.3, rotation)","(x, y) → (−x, −y). P becomes (−2, −1), Q becomes (−6, −1), R becomes (−6, −4). Or: calling it two reflections, one across each axis, counts. (8.G.A.3, rotation)","P becomes (4, 2), Q becomes (12, 2), R becomes (12, 8). The angles stay the same and the shape is the same shape, so the two triangles are similar. Every side length doubles: PQ goes from 4 to 8, QR from 3 to 6, and PR from 5 to 10. The area is multiplied by 2², which is 4: it goes from 6 square units to 24. Partial: the points and the side lengths right with nothing said about the angles or the area is approaching. (8.G.A.3, dilation)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Weigh the Proposal",short:"The Proposal",parts:[{std:["RI.8.8"],dur:145,dir:"Read the proposal to yourself. Then answer out loud.",desc:"delineating an argument and judging whether the reasoning is sound and the evidence relevant and sufficient",html:propText,qs:["What is Vance's claim? What reasons does he give for it?","Which reason rests on the strongest evidence? Explain why that evidence is both relevant and sufficient.","One sentence in the proposal is irrelevant evidence. Which one, and why does it not belong in this argument?","Two parts of the argument rest on weak reasoning. Name one of them and tell me exactly what is wrong with the thinking."],key:["Claim: the bank should cap any member's balance at 40 hours. Reasons: eleven members are hoarding, holding 1,460 hours between them against 1,840 exchanged all year; the 223 hour gap between the top and the bottom of the ledger stops people in deficit from asking; and the research says people who have been helped help more, so a cap would make the top of the page accept help. Needs the claim and at least two reasons. (RI.8.8, delineating the argument)","The first reason. It is relevant because the idle balances are exactly what the cap is meant to change, and sufficient because it gives both figures that matter, 1,460 hours held by eleven members and 1,840 hours exchanged in the year, so a reader can judge the scale without taking his word for it. Note for a careful reader: the two numbers are different kinds of thing, a stored balance against one year of movement, so “against 1,840 hours exchanged” is a comparison of size rather than a share of anything. The second reason is also defensible but rests on an assertion, “Nobody at the bottom asks for help on a page that looks like that,” with no evidence behind it. (RI.8.8, relevant and sufficient evidence)","The sentence about the treasurer having done the job for eleven years without a single error. How well the treasurer keeps the books has nothing to do with whether balances should be capped, so it supports neither the claim nor any of the reasons. (RI.8.8, recognizing irrelevant evidence)","Either one. The Brayford point: a membership rise in the year after a cap does not show the cap caused it, because a year leaves room for many other causes and the proposal rules out none of them. Or the third reason: the research finding is about people who have been helped, and a cap does not get anybody helped. It only stops them earning above 40, so a capped member could simply stop volunteering rather than start accepting help. The proposal also treats a narrow effect, which the article says “tends to be aimed at the same kind of task rather than at helping in general,” as though it applied to helping across the board. Needs the part named and what is wrong with the reasoning. (RI.8.8, soundness of reasoning)"]}]},{id:9,title:"The Longest Side",short:"Pythagoras",parts:[{std:["8.G.B.7"],dur:185,dir:"Use the three pictures. Work on your paper and hold it up. Show the squares you added or subtracted.",desc:"applying the Pythagorean theorem in two and three dimensions, including on the coordinate plane",html:()=>lead("The garden crew works out lengths it cannot reach with a tape."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-wrap:wrap;gap:20px;align-items:flex-start"><div style="text-align:center">${gridFig()}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:2px 0 0">Picture 1</p></div><div style="text-align:center">${ladderFig()}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:2px 0 0">Picture 2</p></div><div style="text-align:center">${crateFig()}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:2px 0 0">Picture 3</p></div></div>`,qs:["Picture 1. Triangle PQR has P at (2, 1), Q at (6, 1) and R at (6, 4). Find the length of PR. Use the coordinates, not a ruler.","Picture 1 again. A straight path runs from (&minus;3, &minus;2) to (5, 4). How long is it?","Picture 2. The ladder is 17 ft long and its foot is 8 ft out from the wall. How far up the wall does the top reach?","Picture 3. The crate is 7 in long, 6 in deep, and 6 in high. What is the longest straight rod that will fit inside it, corner to corner?","A right triangle has legs of 5 cm and 11 cm. Give the hypotenuse exactly, and then to the nearest tenth of a centimeter.","A guy wire 25 m long runs from the top of a 24 m pole down to a stake in the ground. How far from the base of the pole is the stake?"],key:["5 units. The horizontal leg is 6 − 2 = 4 and the vertical leg is 4 − 1 = 3, so 4² + 3² = 16 + 9 = 25 and PR is the square root of 25, which is 5. Or: recognize the 3, 4, 5 triple, or use the distance formula, which is the same theorem written out. (8.G.B.7, distance between two points on the coordinate plane)","10 units. The horizontal leg is 5 − (−3) = 8 and the vertical leg is 4 − (−2) = 6, so 8² + 6² = 64 + 36 = 100 and the path is 10 units long. Or: recognize the 3, 4, 5 triple scaled by 2. Partial: the two legs found with no distance is approaching. (8.G.B.7, distance on the coordinate plane)","15 ft. The ladder is the hypotenuse, so 17² − 8² = 289 − 64 = 225, and the square root of 225 is 15. Watch for adding the squares instead of subtracting. Or: recognize the 8, 15, 17 triple. (8.G.B.7, finding a leg)","11 in. For a box, length² + width² + height² = diagonal², so 7² + 6² + 6² = 49 + 36 + 36 = 121, and the square root of 121 is 11. Or: find the floor diagonal first, √85, then 85 + 36 = 121. Partial: the floor diagonal only is approaching. (8.G.B.7, three dimensions)","Exactly the square root of 146, which is about 12.1 cm. 5² + 11² = 25 + 121 = 146, and the square root of 146 is 12.083, which rounds to 12.1. Partial: one of the exact and the rounded answers given is approaching. (8.G.B.7, an answer that is not a whole number)","7 m. The wire is the hypotenuse, so 25² − 24² = 625 − 576 = 49, and the square root of 49 is 7. Or: recognize the 7, 24, 25 triple. Partial: adding instead of subtracting, giving √1,201, does not count. (8.G.B.7, finding a leg in a real-world problem)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Clause Work",short:"Grammar",parts:[{std:["L.8.1"],dur:140,dir:"A verbal is a verb form doing another job. Answer out loud. When you fix a sentence, say the whole sentence the correct way.",desc:"verbals, active and passive voice, mood, and fixing a shift in voice",qs:["“Copying out the balances took four Saturdays.” Which word is the verbal? What kind is it, and what job does it do in the sentence?","“The member, holding more than a hundred hours, spent nothing.” Find the verbal and name its kind. Who or what does it describe?","“Her plan was to write it in herself.” Find the verbal and name its kind.","Say this sentence in active voice: “The gutters were done by a member of the bank.”","Fix the verb: “I wish the ledger was easier to read.”","Fix the shift in voice: “She wrote the entry, and then the page was copied out by me.”","Name the mood of this sentence: “Check the October column before you file it.”"],key:["Copying. Gerund. With its phrase, “Copying out the balances,” it is the subject of the sentence, so it acts as a noun. (L.8.1a, verbals)","holding. Participle. It describes the member, so it acts as an adjective. (L.8.1a, verbals)","to write. Infinitive. (L.8.1a, verbals)","A member of the bank did the gutters. (L.8.1b, active and passive voice)","I wish the ledger were easier to read. A wish about something that is not true takes were. (L.8.1c, subjunctive mood)","She wrote the entry, and then I copied out the page. Both verbs are now active. (L.8.1d, shift in voice)","Imperative. It gives a command. (L.8.1c, mood)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Explain the Bank",short:"Write",parts:[{std:["W.8.2"],n:7,label:"checks met",dur:220,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative and explanatory writing piece built from a fact box",html:()=>`<p class="big">A neighboring town has asked Westmill to explain how its time bank works and what the November argument is about. Write that explanation. It is not an argument, so do not take a side. Use the fact box. Write 9 to 12 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: the Westmill Time Bank",["<strong>The rule.</strong> One hour of help earns one hour of credit, whatever the help is. An hour of accountancy and an hour of weeding are the same hour. Hours are also earned on the bank's own projects, so the balances do not add up to zero.","<strong>The size.</strong> Fifteen years old. 96 active members. 1,840 hours exchanged last year.","<strong>The pattern.</strong> Eleven members hold more than 100 hours each, 1,460 hours in all, and spend fewer than 5 hours a year. Six members are in deficit. The gap from the top line to the bottom line is 223 hours.","<strong>The argument.</strong> One proposal would cap every balance at 40 hours. Another would leave balances alone and write to members over 100 hours twice a year.","<strong>What the research says.</strong> People who have just watched somebody help are more likely to help, by 5 to 15 percentage points, but the effect is gone by the next day. People who have just been helped help more, for longer, but mostly at the same kind of task.","<strong>What is not known.</strong> The ledger records what was exchanged and when. It has no column for why."])}${checklist(["Introduce the topic and preview what is coming","Organize related information into clear parts, so the rule, the numbers, and the argument do not run together","Develop the topic with facts, definitions, concrete details, and quotations from the fact box","Use transitions that create cohesion and show how the parts relate","Use precise language and the words that belong to the topic: credit, balance, deficit, cap","Keep a formal style and an objective tone, and do not take a side","End with a conclusion that follows from and supports the explanation"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and previews what will follow (W.8.2a)","Organizes ideas into broader categories, so that the rule, the size, the pattern, and the argument are distinguishable rather than mixed together (W.8.2a)","Develops the topic with relevant facts, definitions, concrete details, and quotations from the fact box, with the numbers used accurately: 96 members, 1,840 hours, eleven members, 1,460 hours, 223 hours (W.8.2b)","Explains the argument fairly, giving both the cap and the letter proposal, without arguing for either (W.8.2b)","Uses appropriate transitions to create cohesion and clarify how the parts relate (W.8.2c)","Uses precise language and the vocabulary of the topic, such as credit, balance, deficit, cap, exchange (W.8.2d)","Keeps a formal style and an objective tone and ends with a concluding statement that follows from and supports the explanation (W.8.2e and W.8.2f)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Source Desk",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.8.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:150,dir:"Read both sources. Answer numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Do numbers 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"judging sources, choosing search terms, fixing plagiarism, and citing",html:()=>SOURCES()+`<ol class="qlist"><li>The two sources make very different kinds of claim. Which source would you trust? Give two reasons from the source information itself.</li><li>You want one more source. Which search terms are best, and why? A: helping. B: observed prosocial behavior field experiment duration of effect. C: how to start a time bank.</li><li>Spot the plagiarism. A student's report says, with no quotation marks and no source named: <em>The difference was no longer detectable 24 hours later.</em> Fix it on your paper. Quote it correctly, or paraphrase it and name the author.</li><li>Write a citation for Source A with the author, title, publisher, and year.</li></ol>`,key:["Source A. Two reasons, such as: it names an author with a relevant specialism, it comes from a named institute, it carries a date and is recent, it reports a measured result across four field sites, and it is a replication rather than a single study. Source B is an undated forum post by an unknown user who writes “read that somewhere” and offers no evidence. Full credit for two reasons drawn from the source lines. For the teacher: Source B also asserts that time banks “always collapse,” which is a claim about every case made with no case named. (W.8.8, credibility and accuracy)","B. It uses the key ideas of the research question, so the results will be about watching somebody help, about field studies, and about how long the effect lasts. A is far too broad and C is about setting one up rather than about whether watching works. (W.8.8, search terms)","Either fix earns the check. Quote: Dr. Priya Ramaswamy reports that “the difference was no longer detectable 24 hours later.” Paraphrase: According to Dr. Priya Ramaswamy, the effect had disappeared by the following day. The student must name the source either way, and the quote must carry quotation marks. (W.8.8, quoting or paraphrasing without plagiarism)","Has all four parts. Example: Ramaswamy, Priya. “Observed Helping After Observed Help: A Replication Across Four Field Sites.” Lowell Institute, 2024. Any clear, consistent order is fine. (W.8.8, citation)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Brief the Meeting",short:"Brief",parts:[{std:["SL.8.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:140,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then talk for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"a 1 to 2 minute spoken briefing that presents a claim and findings with evidence from a data table",html:()=>`<p class="big">The November meeting has to choose one of the two proposals below, or neither. Your survey produced the table. Brief the meeting: say what to do, give your strongest evidence, name the drawback of your choice, and end with what you need from the members.</p>${bigTable(["","Cap every balance at 40 hours","No cap, write to members over 100 hours"],[["What changes","no member may hold more than 40","two letters a year to the same members"],["Who it affects now","the 11 members holding more than 100 hours, 1,460 hours in all","the same 11 members"],["Hours above the cap","written off into a community pool","nothing, they stay on the member's line"],["Tried elsewhere","Brayford capped at 50 three years ago and its membership grew 18 percent the next year. No other cause was ruled out.","Castlemaine sent letters in 2024 and reported no change in spending."],["What members here said","3 of the 11 said they would leave","nobody objected"],["Cost to run","one rule change, no volunteer time","two mailings a year, about 6 volunteer hours"],["Worth knowing","the gap from the top line to the bottom line is 223 hours","6 members are in deficit"]],"Two proposals for the November meeting")}<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Say which proposal to adopt, or that neither should be. Give your strongest evidence from the table, name the drawback of your own choice, and end with what you need from the members.</p><div class="row center" style="margin-top:16px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${["My recommendation","The evidence","The drawback","What I need from the meeting"].map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.1rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`,key:["States a clear recommendation up front and stays focused on it. Any of the three positions can be argued. The cap case rests on 1,460 idle hours and the 223 hour gap. The letter case rests on costing nobody their membership against 3 of 11 saying they would leave. The do-nothing case rests on Castlemaine reporting no change from letters and on the Brayford result not being evidence of cause. (SL.8.4)","Emphasizes the most important points in a focused, coherent order rather than reading the whole table out (SL.8.4)","Backs the recommendation with relevant evidence and sound reasoning from the table, with the numbers used accurately. A student who offers Brayford's 18 percent as proof that a cap causes growth has not reasoned soundly: the table says outright that no other cause was ruled out, so it shows only that the two happened in the same two years. (SL.8.4)","Uses well-chosen details, including the drawback of the student's own choice. For the cap: 3 of the 11 said they would leave, and writing hours off into a pool takes something members earned. For letters: Castlemaine reported no change, so the cost is small but so may be the effect. For doing nothing: the 223 hour gap and the 6 members in deficit are left exactly as they are. (SL.8.4)","Appropriate eye contact with the camera, adequate volume, and clear pronunciation for 1 to 2 minutes (SL.8.4)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Where They Disagree",short:"Conflict",parts:[{std:["RI.8.9"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Read the treasurer's reply. Then answer out loud about both texts.",desc:"analyzing two texts that conflict on the same topic and sorting disagreements of fact from disagreements of interpretation",html:()=>replyText()+`<details class="look-back" open style="margin-top:10px"><summary>The proposal</summary>${propText()}</details>`,qs:["Name one point where the two texts disagree about a matter of fact.","Name one point where they disagree about interpretation rather than fact.","What would settle the factual disagreement, and what would not be settled by it?"],key:["Either of two. The 1,460 hours: Vance says the eleven hold it now, Haugen says it is what they have earned since joining and that their balances today total 1,208. Or Brayford: Vance says Brayford capped at 50 three years ago, Haugen says the cap applied only to new members in their first year","What the holding means. Both agree a small group holds a large number of hours. Vance interprets it as hoarding, a cupboard rather than a bank. Haugen does not dispute the pattern, only the figure and the remedy. They also disagree on whether a cap would close the 223 hour gap, which is a prediction, not a fact. Credit the student who says both texts agree on the gap itself","The ledger settles the hours, because it has a balance column and a column of what each member has earned, and the Brayford rule as written settles what Brayford capped. Neither settles whether a cap would work here. For that, Haugen's own point about the bus route shows the problem: two things happening in the same year does not show that one caused the other"]}]},{id:15,title:"Two Short Writes",short:"Writes",parts:[{title:"Argue It",std:["W.8.1"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:60,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a one paragraph argument with a claim, evidence, and a counterclaim",html:()=>lead("Should the bank cap every balance at 40 hours? Write one paragraph. State your claim, give one piece of evidence, and answer the strongest point on the other side.")+checklist(["Begin with your claim","Give one piece of evidence, with the number right","Name the strongest counterclaim and answer it"]),key:["Introduces a clear claim and keeps to it. Either side can be argued (W.8.1a)","Supports it with relevant evidence used accurately, such as the 223 hour gap, the six members in deficit, the 1,208 hours actually held, or the 3 of 11 who said they would leave (W.8.1b)","Acknowledges the strongest counterclaim and answers it rather than the weakest one. The strongest against a cap is that it writes off hours members earned, and that the Brayford result does not show a cap caused anything (W.8.1a and W.8.1b)","One paragraph is the whole job here. Do not ask for more."]},{title:"Write the Moment",std:["W.8.3"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:60,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a one paragraph narrative from a character's point of view",html:()=>lead("Eleven days after Olu Brandt had her gutters done, D. Carrow spent four hours on a boiler. Write one paragraph from Carrow's point of view, about deciding to ask.")+checklist(["Set up where you are and what you know","Use one line of dialogue or one thought","Put the events in order","End the moment"]),key:["Establishes a context and a point of view, with Carrow as the narrator (W.8.3a)","Uses a narrative technique, dialogue, a thought, or description, and sequences the events so one leads to the next (W.8.3b and W.8.3c)","Uses precise words or a sensory detail and ends with a line that follows from the moment rather than stopping mid sentence (W.8.3d and W.8.3e)","Details have to fit the story: the green hardback ledger in the library drawer, the minus eleven on the bottom line, the eight hours written in ink. One paragraph is the whole job here."]}]},{id:16,title:"Repeating and Not",short:"Real Numbers",parts:[{std:["8.NS.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. Write the first answer on your paper.",desc:"rational and irrational numbers and their decimal expansions",qs:["Write 0.444... , where the 4 goes on forever, as a fraction.","What does the decimal expansion of any rational number always do?","Sort these into rational and irrational: 1,840 divided by 96, the square root of 49, and pi."],key:["4/9. Let x = 0.444..., then 10x = 4.444..., so 9x = 4 and x = 4/9. Or: name it from the pattern, since 0.111... is 1/9. Partial: 4/9 with no steps shown is approaching.","It either stops or it repeats a block of digits forever. A decimal that goes on with no repeating block is not rational","1,840 divided by 96 is rational, because it is one whole number over another, and its decimal 19.1666... repeats. The square root of 49 is 7, which is rational. Pi is irrational: its decimal never ends and never repeats. Partial: two of the three sorted right is approaching."]}]},{id:17,title:"Powers and Roots",short:"Exponents",parts:[{title:"Integer Exponents",std:["8.EE.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Solve on your paper. Say your answer.",desc:"applying the properties of integer exponents",qs:["Write 3 to the 4th times 3 to the negative 6th as a single power, then as a fraction.","Simplify 2 cubed, all squared, divided by 2 to the 4th.","Why does 5 to the power 0 equal 1?"],key:["3 to the negative 2, which is 1/9. Add the exponents when the bases match. Or: 81 ÷ 729 = 1/9 worked out as numbers. Partial: the single power right with no fraction is approaching.","4. The top is 2 to the 6th, and 6 take away 4 leaves 2 to the 2nd. Or: 8 squared is 64, and 64 ÷ 16 = 4 worked out as numbers.","Because 5 to the n divided by 5 to the n is 1, and subtracting the exponents gives 5 to the 0. Any non-zero base to the 0 is 1. Or: step down the powers, 125, 25, 5, each step dividing by 5, so after 5 comes 1."]},{title:"Square and Cube Roots",std:["8.EE.A.2"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Solve on your paper. Say your answer.",desc:"evaluating square and cube roots and knowing that the square root of 2 is irrational",qs:["What is the square root of 81, and what is the cube root of 64?","Solve x squared = 49, and solve x cubed = 27.","Is the square root of 2 rational or irrational? How do you know what kind of decimal it has?"],key:["9 and 4. Partial: one of the two right is approaching.","x = 7 or x = negative 7, because both squares give 49. x = 3 only, because a cube keeps the sign. Missing the negative root is the common error here. Partial: x = 7 alone, with x = 3 right, leaves the negative root out and is approaching.","Irrational. It is not a whole number over a whole number, and its decimal 1.41421... never ends and never repeats. Partial: irrational with nothing said about the decimal is approaching."]}]},{id:18,title:"Slope as a Rate",short:"Slope",parts:[{std:["8.EE.B.5"],n:4,dur:140,dir:"Use the graph and the table. Work on your paper and hold it up. Say each answer out loud.",desc:"graphing a proportional relationship, reading the unit rate as the slope of the graph, and comparing two proportional relationships given in different forms",html:()=>lead("Member P's hours from the garden project are on the graph and in the table. Member Q's hours follow the equation h = 3s, where s is Saturdays worked and h is hours earned."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-wrap:wrap;gap:20px;align-items:flex-start">${satGraph()}${satTable()}</div>`,qs:["What is the unit rate for member P, and what is it the slope of?","Draw the same axes on your paper and plot member Q's line, h = 3s. Name two points on Q's line other than (0, 0).","Whose line is steeper, P's or Q's, and what does that steeper slope mean here?","How do you know from the graph and the table that P's relationship is proportional?"],key:["2.5 hours per Saturday. It is the slope of P's line, and it is the same as the constant of proportionality. On the graph the line rises 5 hours across 2 Saturdays, and 5 / 2 = 2.5; in the table 5 / 2, 10 / 4 and 15 / 6 all give 2.5. Or: read the point (1, 2.5) off the line, which on a proportional graph always shows the unit rate. Partial: 2.5 with no account of what it is the slope of is approaching.","A straight line through (0, 0) that climbs 3 hours for every Saturday. Any two points that fit h = 3s count: (1, 3), (2, 6), (4, 12), (6, 18) and (8, 24) are all correct. The line has to start at (0, 0), because no Saturdays worked earns no hours, and it has to sit above P's line everywhere to the right of the origin. Or: plot two points and join them, or step up 3 and across 1 from the origin. Partial: a line drawn with no points named, or two correct points named with no line through the origin, is approaching.","Q's, at 3 hours per Saturday against P's 2.5. Q earns half an hour more for every Saturday worked, so after 8 Saturdays Q has 24 hours and P has 20. Partial: the right line named with nothing said about what the steeper slope means is approaching.","Every pair in the table gives the same quotient, 5 divided by 2, 10 divided by 4, and 15 divided by 6, all 2.5, and on the graph those points lie on one straight line that passes through (0, 0): no Saturdays, no hours. Partial: the constant quotient shown with nothing said about (0, 0) is approaching."]}]},{id:19,title:"Where the Lines Meet",short:"Two Equations",parts:[{std:["8.EE.C.8"],n:4,dur:180,dir:"Use the graph for number 1. Solve the rest on your paper and hold it up. Check each answer in both equations.",desc:"solving pairs of simultaneous linear equations by graph and by algebra, including a word problem and the number of solutions",html:()=>lead("The garden project pays 2 hours to start plus 3 hours a session. The repair table pays 12 hours to start plus 1 hour a session. Both are on the graph."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${sysGraph()}</div>`,qs:["At what point do the two lines cross? What does that point mean for a member choosing between the two jobs?","Solve this pair with algebra: y = 3x &minus; 2 and y = &minus;x + 10.","Without solving, say how many solutions this pair has and how you know: y = 4x + 1 and y = 4x &minus; 6.","The repair table logged 30 jobs last month and spent 96 hours on them. A small job takes 2 hours and a large job takes 5 hours. Write a pair of equations and find how many jobs of each kind there were."],key:["(5, 17). After 5 sessions both jobs have paid 17 hours. Before that the repair table is ahead because of its head start of 12 hours; after that the garden project is ahead because it pays 3 hours a session against 1. Or: solve it instead, 2 + 3s = 12 + s gives 2s = 10, so s = 5 and the total is 17, or table the two totals session by session. Partial: the crossing point read right with no account of what it means is approaching.","x = 3 and y = 7, or (3, 7). Both are already solved for y, so 3x − 2 = −x + 10, which gives 4x = 12 and x = 3, then y = 3(3) − 2 = 7. Check in the other equation: −3 + 10 = 7. Or: substitution, elimination, or graphing both lines all count. Partial: x = 3 with no y is approaching.","No solution. Both slopes are 4 and the y-intercepts differ, so the lines are parallel and never cross, and the solution of a pair is the point where the lines cross. Or: subtract one equation from the other to get 0 = 7, which is impossible. Partial: no solution with no reason is approaching.","18 small jobs and 12 large ones. Equations: s + l = 30 and 2s + 5l = 96. Substituting s = 30 − l gives 60 − 2l + 5l = 96, so 3l = 36 and l = 12, then s = 18. Check: 18 + 12 = 30, and 2(18) + 5(12) = 36 + 60 = 96. Or: elimination, or guess and check against both conditions, both count. Partial: a correct pair of equations with the solving unfinished is approaching."]}]},{id:20,title:"Earned Against Spent",short:"Scatter",parts:[{std:["8.SP.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the scatter plot. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting a scatter plot for association, outliers, and clustering",fig:()=>F.row(scatterFig()),qs:["Describe the association between hours earned and hours spent.","Is there an outlier? Give its coordinates and say what it means about that member.","Is the association linear or nonlinear, and is there any clustering?"],key:["Negative, and fairly strong. As hours earned goes up, hours spent goes down: the members with the largest balances are the ones spending least, which is the pattern the article says the ledger shows. Partial: negative named with no account of what goes down as what goes up is approaching.","Yes, the point at (60, 40). That member has earned 60 hours and spent 40 of them, far more spending than anyone else who has earned about that much, so the point sits well above the line the other nine follow. Partial: the point named with nothing said about that member is approaching.","Roughly linear: the nine points away from the outlier fall close to one straight downward line. The right-hand members cluster low down, between about 4 and 9 hours spent, so the heaviest earners are bunched together at the bottom. Partial: linear answered with nothing said about clustering, or the reverse, is approaching."]}]},{id:21,title:"Why They Came",short:"Colonies",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.1"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"relating the founding motives of the colonies and the Great Awakening to American constitutional democracy",html:()=>`<p class="big">Colonies were founded for different reasons, and several were founded over religion.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Colony</th><th>Founded</th><th>Why</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Virginia</td><td>1607</td><td>by a company, for profit</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Plymouth</td><td>1620</td><td>by Separatists who had left the Church of England, to worship their own way</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Massachusetts Bay</td><td>1630</td><td>by Puritans, to build a society ordered by their own reading of the Bible</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Maryland</td><td>1634</td><td>as a refuge for English Catholics, who were penalized at home</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Rhode Island</td><td>1636</td><td>by Roger Williams, who held that government must not compel belief</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Pennsylvania</td><td>1681</td><td>by William Penn, a Quaker, with toleration written into its laws</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">In the 1730s and 1740s a wave of revivals called the Great Awakening ran through all the colonies. Traveling preachers drew crowds of thousands outdoors, split existing congregations, and told ordinary people that their own judgment about their own salvation outranked the authority of a settled minister.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("heart",74)}${ico("sailboat",74)}</div>`,qs:["Massachusetts Bay and Rhode Island were both founded over religion and they wanted opposite things. Explain the difference.","Four of these colonies were founded by or for a religious group, and each admitted people the others penalized. What practical result did that produce?","The Great Awakening was about salvation, not politics. Why do historians connect it to the Revolution thirty years later?","Name one part of the Constitution or the Bill of Rights that this chart helps explain."],key:["The Puritans of Massachusetts Bay wanted freedom to establish their own faith and to enforce it, so they taxed for the ministry and banished dissenters. Roger Williams, whom they banished, wanted government out of belief altogether, so Rhode Island compelled no one and taxed for no church. Freedom of religion and freedom for one religion are different things, and the colonies held both views at once. Partial: one of the two colonies described is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","No single faith could dominate the whole, so practical toleration grew out of arithmetic rather than agreement: a Quaker was safe in Pennsylvania, a Catholic in Maryland, a Baptist in Rhode Island, and settlers and traders moved among them. By 1787 any national establishment would have meant one group's church imposed on twelve other colonies' worth of objectors, which no one could have carried. Partial: naming the variety with nothing said about why no establishment could have been carried is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","Because it taught enormous numbers of ordinary colonists to judge an established authority for themselves, to form new congregations when they disagreed, and to follow a speaker who held no official position. It also crossed colonial borders, giving scattered colonies a shared experience. Those habits of questioning authority and organizing outside it are the habits the resistance to Parliament ran on. Partial: describing the revival with no link to the habits the resistance ran on is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","Best answer: the First Amendment's bar on an establishment of religion and its protection of free exercise, and Article VI's ban on any religious test for federal office. Accept also freedom of speech, assembly and the press, and the general principle that authority rests on the consent of the governed. Partial: one of the two clauses named is approaching. (HSS-8.1)"]},{std:["HSS-8.2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Bill of Rights and the principle each amendment protects",html:()=>`<p class="big">The first ten amendments, ratified together in 1791.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Amendment</th><th>What it protects</th></tr>
     <tr><td>First</td><td>no establishment of religion, free exercise of religion, speech, press, assembly, petition</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Fourth</td><td>no unreasonable searches; a warrant requires cause</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Fifth</td><td>no second trial for one offense, no being made to testify against yourself, no loss of life, liberty or property without due process</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Sixth</td><td>a speedy public trial, a jury, a lawyer, and the right to confront witnesses</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Eighth</td><td>no cruel and unusual punishment, no excessive bail</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Tenth</td><td>powers not given to the national government stay with the states or the people</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("heart",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["The First Amendment does two separate things about religion. Name both, and explain why a country would want each.","Four of these six amendments are about criminal procedure. What were the framers worried about?","Some Federalists argued a bill of rights was unnecessary and even dangerous. What was their argument, and how was it answered?"],key:["It bars Congress from establishing a religion, and it protects the free exercise of religion. The first keeps the government from putting one faith's authority and the public's taxes behind it, which the colonies had seen produce banishment and penalties. The second keeps the government from interfering with what a person believes and practices. Together they take the question off the government's table in both directions. Partial: one of the two clauses explained is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","About a government using the courts as a weapon: searching houses without cause, holding people without charge, extracting confessions, trying someone again and again until a conviction sticks, and punishing without a hearing. Colonists had met general search warrants and trials moved away from local juries, so these are specific remedies for specific grievances. Partial: listing the rights with no colonial grievance they answer named is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","They argued the national government held only the powers the document listed, so it had no power over speech or religion to begin with, and that writing some rights down would imply the unlisted ones did not exist. The Ninth Amendment answers that worry directly by stating that listing rights does not deny others retained by the people. Partial: naming the objection with nothing said about how the Ninth Amendment answers it is approaching. (HSS-8.2)"]},{std:["HSS-8.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing petition and public comment as means by which citizens change a decision",html:()=>`<p class="big">A county plans to run a four lane road through the middle of a small town. The law requires a comment period and a public hearing before the county board votes. Residents who object form a committee, read the county's own traffic study, find that it counted cars on a holiday weekend, gather 1,800 signatures, bring the author of the study to the hearing to ask about the count, and submit written comments that the board must enter into the record. The board sends the plan back for a new study.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("hands",74)}${ico("truck",74)}${ico("heart",74)}</div>`,qs:["The residents did four different things. Rank them by how much each one mattered, and defend your top choice.","Why does the law require a comment period and a hearing at all, rather than simply letting elected officials decide?","Written comments entered into the record can matter even when the board ignores them at the time. Why?"],key:["Any defended ranking. The strongest case is for finding the flaw in the county's own study, because it gave the board a reason it could act on without appearing to yield to pressure, while signatures show numbers and a hearing shows feeling. A good answer explains that evidence and pressure do different jobs: one supplies the reason, the other supplies the motive. Partial: a ranking with no reasoning is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","Because officials cannot know what a decision will do to the people who live with it, and because a decision people were allowed to contest is easier to accept even when they lose. It also forces the agency to put its reasoning in public where it can be checked, which is how a bad traffic count gets caught. Partial: saying it is fair with nothing said about information or about reasoning being checked is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","Because the record is what a court reviews. A board that approved a plan while a documented objection sat unanswered in its own file has given evidence that the decision was arbitrary, which is the usual ground for overturning one. The record also exists for the next hearing and the next election. Partial: naming the record with nothing said about what a court reviews is approaching. (HSS-8.3)"]}]},{id:22,title:"What the New Nation Hoped",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.4"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing women's aspirations in the new nation and the limits placed on them",html:()=>`<p class="big">In 1776 Abigail Adams wrote to her husband at the Continental Congress asking him to remember the ladies in the new code of laws, and warning that women would not hold themselves bound by laws in which they had no voice. Nothing came of it. What did develop was an argument that mothers raise the next generation of citizens and therefore need real education, and that opened schools: Emma Willard founded a seminary at Troy in 1821 that taught women mathematics, science and history, and Oberlin admitted women to a full college course in 1837. Married women in most states still could not own property, sign a contract, keep their own wages or claim custody of their children.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("heart",74)}${ico("pencil",74)}</div>`,qs:["The argument that mothers raise citizens won schools for women. Explain how an argument that sounds conservative produced a real change.","A woman graduated from Troy in 1830 with a strong education in mathematics and history. What could she do with it, and what could she not?","Abigail Adams's warning in 1776 and the 1848 convention at Seneca Falls make a similar claim. State it."],key:["It worked because it asked for education on grounds no one could object to: if the republic depends on its citizens and mothers form them, an ignorant mother is a public danger. Supporters got the schools they wanted by accepting the framing. The result ran well past the argument, because women taught mathematics, history and rhetoric used them for far more than raising sons. Partial: one of why it worked and what it produced beyond the argument is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","She could teach, write, keep a household's accounts, organize a reform society and educate her own children. She could not vote, serve on a jury, hold office, enter most professions, attend most colleges, or in most states own property or keep her wages once married. The gap between what she was trained to do and what she was permitted to do is what later organized her. Partial: one of the could and the could not lists given is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","That laws binding people who had no voice in making them have no claim on their obedience, which is the Declaration's own argument about consent turned back on the new nation. Both are demands that the country apply its stated principle to women, not requests for a favor. Partial: calling it a request for fair treatment misses the consent argument and is approaching. (HSS-8.4)"]},{std:["HSS-8.5"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Monroe Doctrine as a statement of policy and the power behind it",html:()=>`<p class="big">Between 1810 and 1822 most of Spain's American colonies became independent republics. In 1823 there was talk in Europe of helping Spain take them back. Britain, which had gained a great deal of trade with the new republics, proposed a joint statement with the United States against any such attempt. President Monroe declined the joint version and said it alone, in his annual message to Congress: the Americas were closed to new European colonization, the United States would not interfere in European affairs, and any European attempt to extend its system in this hemisphere would be considered unfriendly. The United States Navy at the time could not have stopped any of it.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("globe",74)}${ico("anchor",74)}${ico("map",74)}</div>`,qs:["The United States could not enforce this. Why state it anyway?","Britain offered to say it jointly and Monroe said it alone. What was gained by refusing?","What actually kept European powers out of the new republics in the 1820s?"],key:["Because a declared position stakes a claim for later, puts other governments on notice, and costs nothing. It also spoke to Americans, defining the hemisphere as the country's proper sphere and Europe as something to stay clear of, which shaped how Americans understood their place long before they could back it up. Partial: one of the foreign and the domestic audience named is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","Independence of judgment, and the credit. A joint statement would have made the United States the junior partner in a British policy and tied it to British aims, which is the entanglement Washington had warned against. Saying it alone made it an American doctrine, which is how it was remembered and invoked for the next century. Partial: naming the independence with nothing said about the credit, or the reverse, is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","The British navy, which had both the power and the commercial interest to keep Spain out and was going to act with or without Monroe. The honest assessment is that the doctrine announced a policy whose enforcement was borrowed, and that it became a real instrument only when the United States itself grew strong enough late in the century. Partial: naming the navy with no honest assessment of the borrowed enforcement is approaching. (HSS-8.5)"]},{std:["HSS-8.6"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing free Black communities in Northeastern cities and the institutions they built",html:()=>`<p class="big">Richard Allen had bought his own freedom in Delaware. In Philadelphia in 1787 he and Absalom Jones walked out of St. George's Methodist church after Black members were ordered into the gallery, and founded their own congregation. In 1816 Allen brought several such congregations together as the African Methodist Episcopal Church, the first denomination in the country governed entirely by Black Americans. Around those churches grew schools, burial societies, mutual aid funds and newspapers. Freedom in the North was still narrow: most states barred Black men from voting, segregated the streetcars and the schools, and shut them out of skilled trades, and after 1850 any Black resident could be seized under the federal fugitive slave law.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("heart",74)}</div>`,qs:["Allen and Jones could have stayed and objected. Why found their own church instead?","Schools, burial societies and mutual aid funds all grew up around the churches. Why would a church be the thing those grew from?","Name two ways Northern freedom was limited, and say what that tells you about the difference between the sections."],key:["Because an institution they ran themselves could not seat them in a gallery, remove their preachers or decide what they were taught. Owning the building, the money and the pulpit meant the terms were theirs, and it let them train ministers and send them to other cities. Objecting would have left the decision in the hands of the people who made it. Partial: naming the walkout with nothing said about owning the terms is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","Because it was the one institution the community owned outright, with a building, a membership that met every week, officers and a collection. In a country where Black Americans were barred from most civic bodies, a church was the available structure for anything collective, from a school to a burial fund to an antislavery meeting. Partial: naming the church with nothing said about what else it was used for is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","Any two: no vote in most states, segregated schools and transport, exclusion from skilled trades and apprenticeships, and the risk of seizure under the 1850 law. The difference between the sections was real and it was a difference of degree in rights rather than a line between freedom and its absence: the North was where Black Americans could own property, publish, organize and sue, and it was not a place of equal citizenship. Partial: one restriction named is approaching, and calling the North equal is the misreading to watch for. (HSS-8.6)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Sold, Traded, Answered",short:"Sections",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.7"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the domestic slave trade and its effect on families in the South",html:()=>`<p class="big">Between about 1810 and 1860 roughly one million people were moved from the Upper South to the Deep South. Almost none of them chose to go.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Upper South: Virginia, Maryland, Kentucky</th><th>Deep South: Alabama, Mississippi, Louisiana, Texas</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Main crops</td><td>tobacco and grain on long worked soil</td><td>cotton and sugar on new land</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Price of a field worker in the 1850s</td><td>lower</td><td>roughly half again as much or more</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Direction of the trade</td><td>people sold away</td><td>people bought in</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Traders bought people at Upper South markets, held them in pens, and moved them south by coastal ship or on foot. Buyers purchased individuals.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("coin",74)}${ico("heart",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain the economics in the chart: why did people get moved in that direction?","Buyers purchased individuals. State plainly what that meant for families, and name one thing people did about it.","Some defenders of slavery said owners cared for enslaved people like family. How does this chart answer that?"],key:["Worn out tobacco land in the Upper South needed fewer workers while new cotton and sugar land in the Deep South was highly profitable and needed many, so prices there were much higher. An Upper South owner could realize more by selling a person than by employing one, which made human beings that region's most valuable export. Partial: naming the price difference with nothing said about the worn land or the new cotton land is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","It meant a husband, a wife, a parent or a child could be sold separately at any moment, with no legal standing to object and often no warning. Historians estimate roughly a third of Upper South marriages were broken by sale. People answered by memorizing family names and places, sending word along the roads, following a sold relative when they could, and after 1865 placing newspaper advertisements and traveling for years to find each other. Partial: one of what it meant and how people answered it is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","It contradicts it directly. A family is not sold at a price set by the cotton market, and an owner who separated a mother from her children to realize a better price in Mississippi was treating them as inventory. The scale makes it impossible to call exceptional: about a million people over fifty years, as a routine business with professional traders, holding pens and shipping lines. Partial: calling the separations exceptional is approaching, since the scale makes them routine. (HSS-8.7)"]},{std:["HSS-8.8"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Santa Fe trade and the mixed society of the Southwest",html:()=>`<p class="big">When Mexico won independence in 1821 it opened trade that Spain had forbidden. American merchants began hauling goods from Missouri to Santa Fe at once.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>The Santa Fe Trail</th><th></th></tr>
     <tr><td>Length</td><td>about 900 miles</td></tr>
     <tr><td>What went south</td><td>cloth, tools, hardware, manufactured goods</td></tr>
     <tr><td>What came north</td><td>silver, mules, furs, wool</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Who traveled it</td><td>American, Mexican and New Mexican traders, with Native guides and trading partners</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Whose land it crossed</td><td>Comanche, Kiowa and Apache territory, by arrangement or at risk</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("horse",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["Spain had banned this trade and Mexico allowed it. Why would the two governments see it so differently?","Americans arrived in Santa Fe as traders, married into New Mexican families and learned Spanish. How did that matter when the United States went to war with Mexico in 1846?","The trail crossed territory held by Native nations who were not party to any of it. What did a trading caravan have to do about that?"],key:["Spain ran a closed imperial system in which its colonies traded only with Spain, so outside merchants were smugglers by definition. Mexico needed revenue and manufactured goods, and Santa Fe was far closer to Missouri than to Mexico City, so legal trade with the north served it. The change is about what each government thought a colony or a province was for. Partial: one of the two governments' policies explained is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","It meant there was already an American commercial community in New Mexico with local connections, information and property to protect, which made the occupation of Santa Fe in 1846 quick and comparatively bloodless. It also meant the United States had a long standing interest in the region before any shot was fired. Partial: naming the traders with nothing said about what that meant in 1846 is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","Negotiate, pay, or take the risk. Caravans sought permission and paid tolls in goods, hired guides, traveled in large groups and timed their crossings, and conflicts followed when they did not. The honest point is that the trail existed on terms set partly by the Comanche and their neighbors, who were a power in the region, not an obstacle on a map. Partial: treating the Comanche as an obstacle rather than a power in the region is approaching. (HSS-8.8)"]},{std:["HSS-8.9"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing David Walker's Appeal and the arguments Black abolitionists made",html:()=>`<p class="big">David Walker was born free in North Carolina and moved to Boston, where he ran a used clothing shop near the docks. In 1829 he printed his Appeal to the Colored Citizens of the World and had sailors sew copies into the lining of clothes bound for Southern ports. The pamphlet argued on two grounds at once: that slavery contradicted the Declaration of Independence, whose words he quoted back at his readers, and that it contradicted the Christianity that slaveholders professed, which he said they had turned upside down. He addressed Black Americans directly rather than appealing to white sympathy, and told them to claim what was already theirs. Several Southern states made circulating it a crime and set a price on him. He died in 1830.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("pencil",74)}${ico("heart",74)}</div>`,qs:["Walker made two arguments at once, one from the Declaration and one from religion. Why use both instead of the stronger one?","He addressed Black readers rather than trying to persuade white readers. What does that choice change about the pamphlet?","Southern legislatures made possessing the pamphlet a crime. What does that reaction tell you about what they thought printed words could do?"],key:["Because his readers and his opponents did not all accept the same authority. A slaveholder who dismissed the Declaration as mere politics still claimed to be a Christian, and one who dismissed the religious charge still claimed the nation's founding creed. Making both arguments left no comfortable place to stand, and each one was turned against the other side's own stated commitments rather than imported from outside. Partial: one of the two arguments explained is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","It makes it a call to action rather than a plea. Most antislavery writing asked white readers to feel pity, which left Black Americans as the subject of someone else's decision; Walker treated them as the people who would act. That is why it alarmed slaveholders far more than a sympathetic appeal would have. Partial: naming the audience with no contrast to appeals for pity is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","That they considered reading and writing a genuine danger to the system, which is consistent with the laws against teaching enslaved people to read. A government that bans a pamphlet is conceding that its position cannot survive the argument, and the effort to intercept copies at the docks shows they expected it to travel. Partial: naming the ban with nothing said about what banning a pamphlet concedes is approaching. (HSS-8.9)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Words, Retreat, Harvest",short:"War and After",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.10"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing how Lincoln restated the purpose of the war in two short speeches",html:()=>`<p class="big">In November 1863 Lincoln spoke for about two minutes at the dedication of the cemetery at Gettysburg. He did not describe the battle. He dated the nation to 1776 rather than to the Constitution, called it dedicated to the proposition that all men are created equal, said the war was a test of whether such a government could endure, and asked the living to finish the work so that the nation should have a new birth of freedom. In March 1865, with the war nearly over, his second inaugural address described slavery as the cause of the conflict, said that both sides had read the same Bible and prayed to the same God, claimed no righteousness for the North, and ended by asking for malice toward none and charity for all.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("star",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("heart",74)}</div>`,qs:["Dating the nation to 1776 instead of 1787 was a deliberate choice. What did it let him argue?","In 1865 he said both sides read the same Bible and prayed to the same God, and he refused to claim righteousness for his own side. Why say that in the week of victory?","Compare what each speech asks of its listeners."],key:["It let him make equality the nation's founding commitment rather than a later addition, since the Declaration says all men are created equal and the Constitution of 1787 accommodated slavery. Starting in 1776 turned the war into the fulfillment of the country's original purpose instead of a change of direction, which is a far stronger argument for asking people to keep fighting. Partial: noting the date with nothing said about equality as the founding commitment is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Because he was already thinking about reunion, and a victory speech that claimed moral superiority would have made reconstruction harder. Saying that both sides appealed to the same faith denied either side the right to call the outcome a divine endorsement, and it set up the request for charity rather than punishment. It is a political choice as much as a moral one. Partial: calling it humility with no account of the political choice is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Gettysburg asks the living to finish an unfinished task, so its demand is effort and resolve directed forward. The second inaugural asks for restraint toward people who were enemies the week before, so its demand is forbearance. One asks listeners to do more, the other asks them to want less in return. Partial: one of the two speeches characterized is approaching. (HSS-8.10)"]},{std:["HSS-8.11"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing how Reconstruction was undone and what replaced it",html:()=>`<p class="big">How the gains of Reconstruction were rolled back.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1872</td><td>Congress lets the Freedmen's Bureau expire</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1873 onward</td><td>a long economic depression turns Northern attention to other things</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1876 to 1877</td><td>a disputed presidential election is settled, and the remaining federal troops leave the South</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1880s and 1890s</td><td>states add poll taxes, literacy tests and grandfather clauses, and organized intimidation goes unpunished</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1896</td><td>in Plessy v. Ferguson the Supreme Court holds that separate facilities may be called equal</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Black registration in Louisiana fell from about 130,000 in 1896 to about 1,300 in 1904.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("clock",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["A grandfather clause exempted anyone whose grandfather had voted from the literacy test. Explain who that helped and who it did not, and why it never mentions race.","The 1873 depression appears on a chart about voting rights. What is it doing there?","Plessy was decided in 1896 under a Constitution that already contained the Fourteenth Amendment. How did the Court reconcile the two, and what did that make possible?"],key:["It exempted white voters, whose grandfathers had been able to vote, and caught Black voters, whose grandfathers had been enslaved and could not. Naming race would have violated the Fifteenth Amendment outright, so the clause used a fact that tracked race perfectly without saying it. It is the clearest example of how a neutral sounding rule can be built to produce a racial result. Partial: describing the clause with nothing said about why it never names race is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","Because enforcement depended on sustained Northern political will, and a depression gave Northern voters and politicians urgent problems of their own. Reconstruction was not defeated only in the South; it was abandoned in the North. Rights on paper need someone willing to spend political capital on them. Partial: blaming the South alone misses the Northern abandonment and is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","The Court held that separating people by race did not by itself deny equal protection as long as the facilities provided were equal, so the amendment was read to permit segregation. In practice the facilities were never equal, and the decision licensed segregation by law in schools, transport, courts and public life for nearly sixty years, until it was overturned. Partial: naming the ruling with nothing said about what followed from it is approaching. (HSS-8.11)"]},{std:["HSS-8.12"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing farmers' economic position after the war and the political movement it produced",html:()=>`<p class="big">Farmers grew more and earned less.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>Price of a bushel of wheat</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1866</td><td>about $1.50</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1880</td><td>about $0.95</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1894</td><td>about $0.49</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Meanwhile farmers borrowed to buy machinery and land, paid whatever the one railroad serving their town charged to ship grain, and paid the elevator operator to store it. The Grange organized cooperative stores and pressed states to regulate rail rates; in the 1890s the Populist party demanded federal control of the railroads, an income tax, and more money in circulation so that debts would be easier to repay.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("truck",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["Prices fell by two thirds while farmers produced more than ever. Explain how both happened at once.","Why would a farmer with a mortgage want more money in circulation?","Farmers were a huge share of the population and still lost most of these fights. Why?"],key:["Because more land, machinery, railroads and foreign competition put far more wheat on the market than demand was growing, and the railroads that let a farmer sell nationally also let every other farmer do the same. Each farmer growing more made sense individually and drove the price down collectively, which is why the answer had to be political rather than agricultural. Partial: naming overproduction with nothing said about why each farmer's choice made sense is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","Because a mortgage is a fixed number of dollars. When there is little money in circulation and prices fall, those dollars are harder to earn, so the real burden of the debt rises every year even though the loan has not changed. More currency would raise crop prices and make the fixed debt easier to repay, which is also exactly why lenders opposed it. Partial: naming the demand with no account of a fixed debt against falling prices is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","Because they were scattered and divided by crop and region while railroads, banks and grain dealers were few, organized, and able to spend on lawyers and legislators. Farmers also competed with each other, which makes collective action hard. A strong answer notices that numbers alone are not political power without organization, money and a shared demand. Partial: naming their numbers with nothing said about organization and money is approaching. (HSS-8.12)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Sugar in the Garden Tomato",short:"Heredity",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing a change in a gene through the protein it codes for to the trait that results",html:()=>`<p class="big">The tomatoes in the club's community garden carry four versions of one gene. It codes for a protein that sits in the cell membranes of the fruit and pumps sugar in.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Gene version</th><th>Protein the gene codes for</th><th>Sugar in the ripe fruit</th></tr>
     <tr><td>S1, unchanged</td><td>full length pump, reaches the membrane, works at the normal rate</td><td>9 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>S2, one base changed in the part that anchors in the membrane</td><td>full length pump that is built but never reaches the membrane</td><td>3 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>S3, two bases changed, two amino acids swapped on an outer loop</td><td>pump reaches the membrane and works at half the rate</td><td>6 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>S4, the end of the gene lost, the last twelve amino acids missing</td><td>pump reaches the membrane and works, but is taken apart quickly</td><td>5 percent</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("herb",74)}</div>`,qs:["S2's protein is complete and the fruit is barely sweet. Say what went wrong and at which stage it went wrong.","Rank S1, S3 and S4 by sugar, and tie each one to what its protein actually does.","A gardener says a gene either works or it does not. Use the chart to answer."],key:["The protein was built in full, so reading the gene was fine. The change sits in the stretch that anchors the pump into the membrane, so the finished protein cannot take its place in the membrane and ends up stuck where it is useless. A pump that is not in the membrane cannot pump, so almost no sugar is loaded and the fruit comes in at 3 percent. The failure is at delivery, between building the protein and the protein reaching its post. Partial: saying the pump does not work, with the failure not placed at delivery between building the protein and it reaching the membrane, is approaching. (LS3)","S1 at 9 percent has a full rate pump in place and kept. S3 at 6 percent has a pump in place and kept but working at half rate, so less sugar is loaded per hour. S4 at 5 percent has a pump that works at full rate but is destroyed early, so there is less of it on duty at any moment. The trait tracks the total pumping the fruit gets, which is the rate multiplied by how much pump is present for how long. Partial: the three versions ranked correctly with the protein's fault tied to only one or two of them is approaching. (LS3)","The chart has four versions of one gene and four different answers, so working and not working is not the choice. S1 works fully. S3 works at half rate. S4 works but does not last. S2 is built and never arrives. Each failure is partial and each has a different cause, which is why the sugar reading is a number rather than a yes or no. Partial: answering that the gardener is wrong with fewer than three of the four versions used to show it is approaching. (LS3)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Hearts and the Park Hillside",short:"Evolution",parts:[{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading a fossil sequence and a graded series of anatomy as evidence of common ancestry",html:()=>`<p class="big">A cut in the park hillside, recorded layer by layer. Layers pile up newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Rock</th><th>Fossils in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1, 0 to 2 m</td><td>topsoil</td><td>frogs and fish like those alive today</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2, 2 to 8 m</td><td>shale</td><td>an amphibian with four limbs, ribs and a flat skull</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3, 8 to 18 m</td><td>sandstone</td><td>an animal with stout fin bones, a neck, and both gills and simple lungs</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4, 18 to 30 m</td><td>limestone</td><td>a lobe finned fish with gills only and no neck</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The heart in four living animals.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Animal</th><th>Chambers in the heart</th><th>How blood from the lungs or gills is kept separate</th></tr>
     <tr><td>fish</td><td>2</td><td>blood goes once through the gills, then straight to the body</td></tr>
     <tr><td>frog</td><td>3</td><td>lung blood and body blood mix in one ventricle</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lizard</td><td>3, with a partial wall</td><td>partly separated by the wall</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bird</td><td>4</td><td>fully separated into two sides</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("frog",74)}${ico("fish",74)}${ico("heart",74)}</div>`,qs:["Read the layers from oldest to youngest and say what appears in what order.","Describe the pattern in the heart chart, and say why the two three chambered hearts matter to the argument.","A chicken embryo's heart begins as a single tube, becomes two chambered, then three, then four. Say what that adds to the two charts."],key:["Oldest first: layer 4, a lobe finned fish with gills only and no neck; layer 3, an animal with stout fin bones, a neck, and both gills and simple lungs; layer 2, an amphibian with four limbs, ribs and a flat skull; layer 1, the living frogs and fish. Fins become stout and load bearing, a neck appears, lungs appear alongside gills, and only then do true limbs and ribs appear. Each step is present in the layer below before it is finished in the layer above, and the sequence runs in one direction through time. Partial: the four layers in the right order with nothing said about each step being present below before it is finished above is approaching. (LS4)","The chambers run from two in a fish, to three in a frog, to three with a partial wall in a lizard, to four fully separated in a bird, and the separation of lung blood from body blood improves along the same run. The three chambered hearts matter because they are the halfway steps. If each animal's heart were simply the heart it needs, there would be no reason for intermediate forms to exist at all. A graded series is what a structure modified step by step from a shared starting point looks like. Partial: describing the run from two to four chambers with nothing said about why the halfway hearts matter is approaching. (LS4)","The chicken's own development passes through the same series the chart lays out across living animals, so the four chambered heart is built by adding to a plan that is still the two chambered plan at the start. That is only expected if the instructions were inherited from ancestors whose hearts stopped at the earlier stages. The hillside gives the sequence in time, the heart chart gives the graded series across living animals, and the embryo shows the earlier stages still being built. All three agree. Partial: saying the chicken heart passes through the same stages, with nothing said about the four chambered heart being built by adding to an earlier plan, is approaching. (LS4)"]},{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both sets of numbers. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"explaining natural selection from a trait distribution and distinguishing it from artificial selection",html:()=>`<p class="big">Clover grows across the park. The long tongued bee that used to work it has gone from the district, and only short tongued bees are left, which cannot reach into a deep flower. Two hundred plants were measured each generation.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Length of the flower tube</th><th>Generation 1</th><th>Generation 6</th><th>Generation 13</th></tr>
     <tr><td>under 6 mm</td><td>36</td><td>58</td><td>108</td></tr>
     <tr><td>6 to 9 mm</td><td>76</td><td>93</td><td>78</td></tr>
     <tr><td>over 9 mm</td><td>88</td><td>49</td><td>14</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The club's own seed saving is a different story. For twenty years members kept seed only from the sunflowers that carried the most flower heads. The average went from one head a plant to seven.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("bee",74)}${ico("purpleFlower",74)}${ico("sunflower",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain the shift in the chart. Name the trait that varies, what is doing the selecting, and what has to be inherited.","Work out the shares over 9 mm in generation 1 and in generation 13, then say what would happen if the long tongued bee came back to the district.","Compare the clover with the club's sunflowers, and name one way that selecting hard for flower heads could leave the sunflowers worse off."],key:["Flower tube length already varies among the clover plants and is inherited. Only short tongued bees are left, and they cannot reach nectar in a deep tube, so deep flowered plants get fewer visits, are pollinated less and set less seed, while shallow flowered plants are pollinated and set seed normally. The next generation is grown mostly from shallow flowered parents, so the distribution shifts shallow, from 36 under 6 mm to 108. The bees do not shorten any plant's flowers; they decide which plants leave seed. Partial: two of the three steps given is approaching, and the inheritance step is usually the one left out. (LS4)","Generation 1: 88 of 200, which is 44 percent. Generation 13: 14 of 200, which is 7 percent. If the long tongued bee returned, deep flowers would be pollinated again, and since deep tubes also hold more nectar and may attract more visits, the selection could reverse and the deep end could grow back, as long as any plants with the gene versions for deep tubes are left. At 14 in 200 there are still some, which is why losing variation entirely is the serious risk. Partial: working out the 44 and 7 percent with no prediction made about the long tongued bee returning is approaching. (LS4)","In the park nothing chooses: the bees that happen to be there decide which plants reproduce, which is natural selection. With the sunflowers the members choose the parents directly, which is artificial selection, and twenty years of it took the average from one head to seven. The cost is that breeding from a handful of plants every year narrows the variation in the whole line, so gene versions nobody was looking at get dropped, and the sunflowers can end up with weaker stems, later flowering or less resistance to mildew even while the head count climbs. Partial: naming the selecting agent in both cases with nothing named that the sunflower line could lose is approaching. (LS4)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Where the Sun Comes Up",short:"Universe",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the log. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the yearly cycle from sunrise observations and naming gravity's part in orbits",html:()=>`<p class="big">A log kept at the community garden gate for a year.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Where on the horizon the sun came up</th><th>Time</th></tr>
     <tr><td>March 20</td><td>due east</td><td>6:58 a.m.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>June 21</td><td>well north of east</td><td>5:42 a.m.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>September 22</td><td>due east</td><td>6:55 a.m.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>December 21</td><td>well south of east</td><td>7:21 a.m.</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sun",74)}${ico("purpleFlower",74)}${ico("compass",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain why the sunrise point travels along the horizon through the year, and why it comes back to due east twice.","The moon always shows Earth the same face. Say what that means about how the moon turns, and name the cycle its turning matches.","Name the force holding the moon to Earth and Earth to the sun, then say what two measurements you would need in order to compare the two pulls."],key:["Earth's axis is tilted about 23.5 degrees and keeps pointing the same way in space while Earth travels around the sun. That carries the sun's apparent path north of the equator for half the year and south of it for the other half, so the point where it clears the horizon slides north and then south and back. Twice a year Earth's orbit brings it to the position where neither hemisphere leans toward the sun, and on those two days the sun rises due east everywhere, which is why due east appears in March and again in September. Partial: naming the tilt as the cause of the traveling sunrise with nothing said about why due east comes round twice is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","It means the moon does turn, once on its own axis for every one trip around Earth, which is exactly what keeps the same face toward us. A moon that did not turn at all would show us every side in the course of an orbit. The turning matches the orbit, about 27.3 days, and Earth's gravity acting unevenly across the moon is what locked the two together over a long time. Partial: answering that the moon does not turn is the misconception this question is written to catch; saying it turns once an orbit with no cycle named is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","Gravity in both cases. To compare the two pulls you would need the masses of the bodies doing the pulling, Earth and the sun, and the distances between the orbiting body and each of them, because gravitational force grows with the masses and weakens with the square of the distance. The sun holds about 333,000 times Earth's mass, which pulls the comparison one way, and it sits about 390 times further from the moon than Earth does, which pulls it the other way, so both measurements are needed and neither settles it alone. Partial: naming gravity and one of the two measurements is approaching, and the distances are usually the ones left out. (MS-ESS1)"]},{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"comparing solar system masses and using a fossil to match layers between distant places",html:()=>`<p class="big">Masses, each given as a multiple of Earth's.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Object</th><th>Mass compared with Earth</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the sun</td><td>333,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Jupiter</td><td>318</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Earth</td><td>1</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the moon</td><td>0.012</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Ceres, the largest asteroid</td><td>0.00016</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The park hillside again, dated, with the fossil each layer is recognized by.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Unit, top to bottom</th><th>Age of the rock</th><th>Fossil it is recognized by</th></tr>
     <tr><td>topsoil</td><td>3,000 years</td><td>none</td></tr>
     <tr><td>shale</td><td>365 million years</td><td>the flat skulled amphibian</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sandstone</td><td>375 million years</td><td>the stout finned animal with a neck</td></tr>
     <tr><td>limestone</td><td>390 million years</td><td>the lobe finned fish</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("globe",74)}${ico("sun",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}</div>`,qs:["Use the masses. Say how many Earths and how many Jupiters the sun is worth, and what that means for whose gravity sets the shape of the solar system.","Add the five masses and work out the share held by the sun alone. Say what your answer explains.","The same stout finned animal with a neck is found in sandstone on two other continents. Say what that lets a geologist do, and name one thing it does not establish."],key:["The sun is 333,000 Earths. In Jupiters it is 333,000 divided by 318, which is about 1,050 Jupiters. So the sun outweighs the largest planet by a thousand times, which means the sun's gravity dominates the whole system: every planet orbits the sun rather than one another, and the planets' pulls on each other only nudge those orbits. Partial: giving the 333,000 Earths with the Jupiter figure not worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","The five add to about 333,319 Earth masses, and 333,000 divided by 333,319 is about 0.999, so the sun holds about 99.9 percent of the total. That explains why the solar system is a set of small bodies going around one large one, why the planets' paths are so close to simple closed orbits, and why the sun barely moves while everything else does. Partial: working out the 99.9 percent with nothing said about what it explains is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","It lets a geologist say the sandstone at all three places formed during the same stretch of time, because that species existed only for a limited span and its fossils can only be buried while it was alive. That is how layers are matched between places too far apart to trace a bed across the ground. It does not establish that the three places had the same climate, or that the rock formed in the same way, or that they were close together when it formed. Same age is all a shared fossil gives. Partial: saying the three layers are the same age with nothing named that a shared fossil does not establish is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]}]},{id:28,title:"What the Town Throws Out",short:"Human Impact",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS3"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"separating population growth from per person consumption in total demand on a resource",html:()=>`<p class="big">Packaging in this town, from the collection weighbridge. The sorting line can process 700 tons a year; the rest is buried.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>People</th><th>kg per person per year</th><th>Total</th></tr>
     <tr><td>2000</td><td>9,000</td><td>38</td><td>342 tons</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2010</td><td>13,000</td><td>52</td><td>676 tons</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2020</td><td>18,000</td><td>61</td><td>1,098 tons</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2025</td><td>20,000</td><td>55</td><td>1,100 tons</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("box",74)}${ico("truck",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["Total packaging more than trebled from 2000 to 2025. Work out how much of that is more people and how much is more per person.","Work out how many tons are buried in 2025, and the per person figure that would end the burying altogether.","Name two effects on Earth's systems of burying that much every year for decades, and say what the chart shows about asking people to use less compared with the town growing."],key:["People rose from 9,000 to 20,000, a factor of about 2.22. Per person use rose from 38 kg to 55 kg, a factor of about 1.45. Multiplied together, 2.22 times 1.45 is about 3.2, and 342 times 3.2 is about 1,100 tons, which matches. Both factors pushed the total up this time, with population the larger of the two but per person use adding nearly half again on top of it. Partial: naming both factors with only one of the two multipliers worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","1,100 tons produced less 700 tons processed leaves 400 tons buried a year. To end the burying, the total would have to come down to 700 tons, which among 20,000 people is 700,000 divided by 20,000, or 35 kg each. That is below even the 38 kg of 2000, so the town would have to use less packaging per person than it did twenty five years ago while holding twice the people. Partial: getting the 400 tons buried with the 35 kg figure not worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","Any two: buried packaging takes up land that cannot be used for anything else for a long time; plastics break into small fragments that move into soil, groundwater and from there into streams and living things; buried paper and food packaging release methane as it breaks down, which adds to the gases warming the atmosphere; and every ton buried is a ton of material and energy taken from the Earth and not recovered. The chart shows per person use can be cut, since it came down from 61 kg to 55 kg, but the population column has risen in every row, and at 2.22 times the people a cut per person has to be much larger to show up in the total at all. Partial: two effects named with nothing said about a cut per person having to be much larger once the population has doubled is approaching. (MS-ESS3)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Wagons at the Food Drive",short:"Forces",parts:[{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"applying the third law to a push between two objects and relating force and mass to motion",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two wagons stand back to back on the hall's smooth floor with a squeezed spring between them. The spring is let go.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Wagon</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed just after release</th></tr>
     <tr><td>loaded wagon</td><td>60 kg</td><td>0.5 m/s</td></tr>
     <tr><td>empty wagon</td><td>20 kg</td><td>1.5 m/s</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">One wagon is then pushed across the floor with a measured force.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Force</th><th>Mass</th><th>Acceleration</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>36 N</td><td>18 kg</td><td>2 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>36 N</td><td>9 kg</td><td>4 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>108 N</td><td>18 kg</td><td>6 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>84 N</td><td>42 kg</td><td>2 m/s each second</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("box",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("truck",74)}</div>`,qs:["Say what each wagon experienced when the spring was let go, using Newton's third law, and compare the two forces.","Multiply mass by speed for each wagon. Say what the two products show, then work out the empty wagon's speed if the loaded one had massed 100 kg and still left at 0.5 m/s.","Which two trials differ only in the mass, and which only in the force? Say what each pair shows, then confirm trial 4 with the rule."],key:["The spring pushed one way on the loaded wagon and the opposite way on the empty one, with forces equal in size, at the same instant and for the same length of time. The heavier wagon is not pushed harder and does not push harder; the pair of forces is one event with two ends. Both wagons roll, so both move. Partial: saying both wagons were pushed apart, with nothing said about the heavier wagon not being pushed harder, is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Loaded: 60 times 0.5 is 30. Empty: 20 times 1.5 is also 30. The two quantities of motion match in size and are opposite in direction, which is what equal and opposite forces acting for the same time give. A 100 kg loaded wagon at 0.5 m/s carries 50, so the 20 kg wagon would leave at 50 divided by 20, which is 2.5 m/s. Partial: finding both products equal to 30 with the 2.5 m/s prediction not worked out is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Trials 1 and 2 differ only in mass, 18 kg against 9 kg at 36 N, and the acceleration doubles from 2 to 4, so half the mass gives twice the acceleration. Trials 1 and 3 differ only in force, 36 N against 108 N at 18 kg, and the acceleration trebles from 2 to 6, so three times the force gives three times the acceleration. Trial 4 confirms it: 42 kg times 2 m/s each second is 84 N. Partial: naming both pairs of trials with trial 4 not checked against force equals mass times acceleration is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]},{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying what changes a magnetic force, naming the field in a gap, and relating weight to mass",html:()=>`<p class="big">Magnets hold notices on the club's steel board. The pull was measured at three gaps, with one magnet and with two stacked.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Magnets</th><th>Pull at 5 mm</th><th>Pull at 10 mm</th><th>Pull at 20 mm</th></tr>
     <tr><td>one</td><td>2.40 N</td><td>0.60 N</td><td>0.15 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>two stacked</td><td>4.60 N</td><td>1.15 N</td><td>0.29 N</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The same 2.5 kg donation tin, weighed in three places.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Where</th><th>Weight on the scale</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the club hall</td><td>24.5 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on the moon</td><td>4.1 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on Venus</td><td>22.2 N</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("heart",74)}${ico("key",74)}${ico("moneybag",74)}</div>`,qs:["Describe both patterns in the magnet chart, the one along each row and the one down each column, with numbers.","Name what lies between a magnet and the steel board while they are apart, and say what slipping a sheet of paper between them does to the pull and why.","Explain the three donation tin weights, and say which single fact about a world decides most of the answer."],key:["Along a row, the pull falls fast with the gap: one magnet gives 2.40 N at 5 mm, 0.60 N at 10 mm and 0.15 N at 20 mm, so doubling the gap cuts the pull to a quarter and quadrupling it cuts it to a sixteenth. Down a column, stacking a second magnet roughly doubles the pull at every gap, 2.40 to 4.60 N and 0.60 to 1.15 N. So strength scales the whole curve up and distance governs its shape. Partial: describing one of the two patterns with figures is approaching, and the column pattern is usually the one left out. (MS-PS2)","A magnetic field, which fills the space around the magnet and acts on the steel where the steel is, with nothing having to touch. Paper is not magnetic, so it does almost nothing to the field itself; what it does is hold the magnet further from the steel. A sheet is about 0.1 mm, so the change is small, but a stack of twenty notices adds about 2 mm, and the chart shows that going from 5 mm to 10 mm already costs three quarters of the pull. The notice falls off because of the gap, not because paper blocks magnetism. Partial: saying the paper blocks the magnetism is the misconception this question is written to catch; naming the field and the added gap with no figure from the chart is approaching. (MS-PS2)","The tin holds the same 2.5 kg of matter in all three places. Weight is the gravitational force on it, set by its mass, the mass of the world, and the distance to that world's center. The moon's small mass gives 4.1 N. Venus has almost Earth's mass and almost Earth's radius, so it gives 22.2 N, nearly the hall's 24.5 N. The mass of the world is what decides most of it, with the radius deciding the rest, which is why Venus is close to Earth and the moon is not. Partial: explaining two of the three weights, or all three with no judgment made about mass being the deciding fact, is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Shelves in the Hall",short:"Energy",parts:[{std:["MS-PS3"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"relating kinetic energy to mass and speed and stored energy to the arrangement of objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">A wagon's kinetic energy was measured as it crossed the hall.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed</th><th>Kinetic energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>12 kg</td><td>1 m/s</td><td>6 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>12 kg</td><td>3 m/s</td><td>54 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>36 kg</td><td>1 m/s</td><td>18 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>12 kg</td><td>5 m/s</td><td>150 J</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Stored energy measured for a 15 kg box of tins on three shelves, and for the spring in the hall door closer.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>Stored energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>box on the 1.4 m shelf</td><td>205.8 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>box on the 2.8 m shelf</td><td>411.6 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>box on the 4.2 m shelf</td><td>617.4 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>door closer spring pushed in 5 cm</td><td>1.25 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>door closer spring pushed in 10 cm</td><td>5.00 J</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("box",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["Trial 2 trebles trial 1's speed and trial 3 trebles trial 1's mass. Compare what each one did to the energy.","Work out the kinetic energy of a 36 kg box moving at 5 m/s, and say which trials you used to get there.","Use the shelf rows. State the rule, then explain how the same box on the same shelf could honestly be given a different number.","Say which of the two stores doubles when its distance doubles and which quadruples, then explain the difference in terms of the force involved."],key:["Trebling the speed took the energy from 6 J to 54 J, a factor of nine. Trebling the mass took it from 6 J to 18 J, a factor of three. The same factor applied to speed multiplies the energy by the square of it, while applied to mass it multiplies the energy by the factor itself, so speed is by far the stronger of the two. Partial: naming both factors of nine and three with nothing said about speed being squared while mass is not is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Trial 3 gives 18 J for 36 kg at 1 m/s. Going from 1 m/s to 5 m/s is five times the speed, so twenty five times the energy: 18 times 25 is 450 J. The same answer comes from trial 4, 150 J for 12 kg at 5 m/s, trebled for three times the mass, which is also 450 J. Either route works, which is a good check. Partial: reaching 450 J by one route with no trials named, or a figure of 90 J that scales the speed once rather than squaring it, is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Stored energy is proportional to the shelf height: 1.4 m gives 205.8 J, twice the height gives twice the energy at 411.6 J, three times gives 617.4 J. The numbers in the chart are measured from the hall floor. Measured from the top of the 4.2 m shelf, the box on the 1.4 m shelf would be given a negative figure, and measured from the cellar below it would be given a larger one. All three are honest, because stored energy belongs to an arrangement of the box and Earth and is always relative to a position somebody chose. Partial: stating the proportion to height with nothing said about the hall floor being a chosen reference is approaching. (MS-PS3)","The shelf store doubles when the height doubles. The spring store quadruples when the push doubles, 1.25 J to 5.00 J. Earth pulls the box with practically the same force at every height in the hall, so each extra meter stores the same amount and the total is proportional to the height. The spring pushes back harder the further it is compressed, so each extra centimeter stores more than the last and the total goes up with the square. How the force changes as the parts move is what sets the rule. Partial: naming which store doubles and which quadruples with nothing said about the spring pushing back harder the further it is compressed is approaching. (MS-PS3)"]}]},{id:31,title:"The Piano in the Hall",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"relating amplitude to the energy in a wave and using wavelength and frequency to find wave speed",html:()=>`<p class="big">One piano string was plucked four ways and a meter at the far end of the hall recorded the energy arriving.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Pluck</th><th>How far the string was pulled aside</th><th>Wavelength of the sound</th><th>Cycles per second</th><th>Energy at the meter</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A</td><td>1 mm</td><td>1.550 m</td><td>220</td><td>1 unit</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B</td><td>2 mm</td><td>1.550 m</td><td>220</td><td>4 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C</td><td>4 mm</td><td>1.550 m</td><td>220</td><td>16 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D</td><td>4 mm</td><td>0.775 m</td><td>440</td><td>16 units</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("megaphone",74)}${ico("heart",74)}</div>`,qs:["Plucks A, B and C differ only in how far the string was pulled aside. State the relationship between that distance and the energy.","Pluck D is the same string stopped halfway, which sounds an octave higher. Work out the speed from rows C and D and say why the two agree.","A listener says pluck D must carry more energy because it sounds higher. Use the chart to answer."],key:["Each doubling of the pull multiplies the energy by four: 1 unit at 1 mm, 4 units at 2 mm, 16 units at 4 mm. Energy goes up with the square of the amplitude, so four times the pull gives sixteen times the energy. Amplitude is the property that carries a wave's energy. Partial: saying a harder pluck is louder, with nothing said about the energy going up with the square of the amplitude, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Speed is wavelength times cycles per second. C: 1.550 m times 220 is 341 m per second. D: 0.775 m times 440 is also 341 m per second. The speed is a property of the air in the hall, not of the string, so halving the wavelength forces the frequency to double, which is exactly what an octave up means. Partial: working out one of the two speeds, or both with nothing said about the air rather than the string setting the speed, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","No. C and D both pulled the string 4 mm and both delivered 16 units at the meter, so the energy is identical while the pitch is an octave apart. Pitch comes from the frequency and energy comes from the amplitude, and the chart varies them separately on purpose. A high note can be faint and a low note can be powerful. Partial: answering no with the identical 4 mm pull and 16 units not quoted from the chart is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]},{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Use the chart and the two methods. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading reflection, absorption and transmission from data and comparing a digital with an analog signal",html:()=>`<p class="big">A lamp sends 100 units of light at each material. One meter sits behind it and one beside the lamp.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Material</th><th>Passed through</th><th>Came back</th><th>Neither</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the hall's window glass</td><td>89</td><td>8</td><td>3</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the red stage curtain</td><td>2</td><td>24</td><td>74</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a white bedsheet held up</td><td>29</td><td>59</td><td>12</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a mirror</td><td>0</td><td>94</td><td>6</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a thin sheet of red plastic</td><td>31</td><td>9</td><td>60</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Two ways to get the club's volunteer hours to the regional office.<br>Method 1: a dial on the phone line whose angle stands for the number of hours.<br>Method 2: the number typed as digits and sent as a text message.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("phone",74)}</div>`,qs:["The mirror sends back 94 and the white sheet 59, and both look pale. Say what each one does with the light and why only one shows an image.","The red plastic passes 31 and absorbs 60. Say which colors it is absorbing, and how the chart lets you know.","The line crackles and drops out for a moment. Say which method still delivers the right number of hours and why, then name one way the other method could be made to survive the same fault."],key:["The mirror reflects 94 of 100 and reflects it in an orderly way, so every ray leaves at a matching angle and the pattern of light is preserved, which is what an image is. The sheet reflects 59 and scatters it in every direction, so the light comes back but the pattern does not, and the sheet looks evenly bright with no image in it. Brightness and image are two different things, and only the orderliness of the reflection decides the second. Partial: contrasting the 94 with the 59, with nothing said about the mirror reflecting in an orderly way, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","It is absorbing the blue and green light. We know because it looks red, which means red is what reaches the eye, and the chart says 60 of 100 units were absorbed. White light from the lamp is a mixture, and 31 passed through while 60 was taken up, so the parts that did not pass are the parts that are missing from what comes out. A filter makes a color by removing the others, not by adding its own. Partial: naming the absorbed colors with nothing said about a filter making a color by removing the others is approaching. (MS-PS4)","The text message. It is a digital signal: the digits are sent as patterns that only have to be told apart from each other, and if a drop out loses part of the message the system can resend it until it arrives whole. The dial is analog, where the angle itself is the value, so crackle moves the needle and a drop out leaves the office reading whatever the dial settled on, with no sign it is wrong. The dial could be made to survive by sending the reading several times and comparing, or by adding a second dial as a check, but it would still not reveal which reading was the true one. Partial: choosing the text message with nothing said about why the dial hides its own error, or with no way named to make the dial survive, is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]}]},{id:32,title:"Telling Everyone in an Hour",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Read the situation and the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria and constraints and evaluating competing designs against them",html:()=>`<p class="big">The club wants a way to tell its members when a neighbor needs help within the hour. There are 140 members and $500 a year. Nobody can be on call all day, some members have no smartphone, and nothing may give out a neighbor's address before someone has offered to help. The club has asked that at least 100 members be reachable within the hour.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Cost a year</th><th>Set up</th><th>Reachable in an hour</th><th>Works without a smartphone</th><th>Gives the address before anyone volunteers</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: a group text list</td><td>$90</td><td>3 hours</td><td>118 of 140</td><td>yes, any phone</td><td>yes, the first message names the street</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: a club app</td><td>$420</td><td>30 hours</td><td>96 of 140</td><td>no</td><td>no, the address appears once a member accepts</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: a phone tree, each member calling two others</td><td>$0</td><td>6 hours</td><td>74 of 140</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D: a paid answering service</td><td>$2,600</td><td>2 hours</td><td>131 of 140</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("phone",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("heart",74)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem, then separate the criteria from the constraints, and name the constraint that no amount of good performance can buy off.","Go through the chart and say what each of the four designs fails on.","Nothing in the chart meets everything. Say which two designs you would combine, exactly how, and which failure your combination fixes."],key:["Achieve: get a request for an hour's help from one neighbor to enough members, fast enough to be answered within the hour. Criterion, the measure of success: how many of the 140 members are reachable within the hour, with 100 as the level the club set. Constraints, the limits on any solution: no more than $500 a year, no member on call all day, it must work for members with no smartphone, and no address given out before someone volunteers. The address rule is the constraint no performance can buy off: a design that reaches all 140 but publishes a neighbor's address has broken the promise the club made to the person asking for help. Partial: the criteria and constraints separated with the address rule not picked out as the one no performance can buy off is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","A fails the address constraint: its first message names the street. B fails the no smartphone constraint, and at 96 of 140 it also falls just short of the 100 the club asked for. C fails the criterion badly at 74 of 140, though it breaks no constraint and costs nothing. D fails the budget at $2,600 against $500. Every design on the chart fails something, which is the usual situation rather than a mistake in the chart. Partial: two or three of the four designs judged correctly is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Any defensible combination. The strongest is B with C: members with smartphones get the app, which keeps the address private until someone accepts, and the rest are reached by the phone tree, which costs nothing and needs no smartphone. Together they reach more than either alone and the app's 96 plus the tree's reach among the members the app misses should clear 100, which fixes both B's smartphone failure and C's shortfall. A second option is to keep design A and change its wording so the first message asks for a reply and only then sends the address, which fixes A's single failure for no money at all, and that is often the better engineering answer: change the design rather than replace it. Partial: a combination named with no account of which failure it fixes is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]},{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing test data to find which change helps and planning the next round of testing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four ways of sending the request were tried, forty requests each.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Version</th><th>Who was contacted</th><th>What the first message said</th><th>Requests answered within the hour, out of 40</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>all 140 at once</td><td>named the street straight away</td><td>9</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>all 140 at once</td><td>asked for a reply before giving the address</td><td>21</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>the 20 nearest members only</td><td>asked for a reply before giving the address</td><td>14</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>the 20 nearest first, then everyone after 20 minutes</td><td>asked for a reply before giving the address</td><td>27</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("phone",74)}${ico("clock",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name the one change between versions 1 and 2, and between 2 and 3, and what each did to the number answered.","Version 3 did worse than version 2 and version 4 did best of all. Explain what version 4 took from each of them.","Specify a fifth version and predict its result, then name a variable none of the four runs changed and say how you would test it."],key:["Versions 1 to 2: only the wording changed, from naming the street to asking for a reply first, and the answers rose from 9 to 21 out of 40. Versions 2 to 3: only the group contacted changed, from all 140 to the 20 nearest, and the answers fell from 21 to 14. One variable at a time, which is why each change can be credited with its result. Partial: naming both changes with the rise to 21 or the fall to 14 not quoted is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Version 3 asked fewer people, so fewer could say yes: 20 members cannot supply more help than 140, however well chosen they are. Version 4 kept the better wording from version 2, kept the idea from version 3 that the nearest members are the most likely to come, and then removed version 3's limitation by widening to everyone after twenty minutes. It gets the speed of a short list and the reach of a long one, which is why it beat both at 27 of 40. Partial: accounting for version 3's poor result with nothing said about what version 4 took from each of the earlier two is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Any version argued from the chart. A good fifth version keeps version 4's two stage sending and shortens the wait from 20 minutes to 10, predicting a little better than 27 out of 40 on the grounds that the second wave arrives sooner inside the hour, while watching whether the nearest members stop replying because the wider list gets there first. The variable none of the four changed is the time of day the request went out, since a weekday morning and a weekday evening are very different for 140 volunteers, so the next test should hold version 4's settings and send twenty requests in the morning and twenty in the evening. Partial: a fifth version argued from the chart with the time of day not identified as the variable never changed is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={1:{id:"L1",title:"Level Up: Strong and Thorough",std:["RL.9-10.1"],needs:["RL.8.1"],n:1,dir:"Answer out loud. Read me the exact words for each detail.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Claim: the narrator's judgment of her grandmother changes over the course of the story, and the story shows the change without ever announcing it. Prove it with strong and thorough evidence. Give at least three details from different parts of the story, and explain what each one shows.</p>${story()}`,key:["Three or more details from across the story, each explained. Examples: the job of copying the balances “took me four Saturdays to stop resenting,” which is the narrator saying outright that her first reading of all this was grievance. “You could have the gutters done. Twice,” which treats the hours as a shopping list and her grandmother as simply stubborn. “If nobody spends, then it is not a bank,” which is a sharper reading but still one about the system rather than the person. “I did not have an answer that night. I have one now, and it is not a comfortable one,” which marks the change explicitly as a change in the narrator. The account of the fifteen years, which is a judgment the fourteen-year-old could not have made. And the last three paragraphs, where she credits her grandmother with an effect she cannot prove and will not pretend to. (HS RL.9-10.1, strong and thorough textual evidence)"]},2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: f of x",std:["F-IF.1"],needs:["8.F.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Use function notation in every answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The balance rule is f(s) = 212 &minus; s, where s is the number of hours spent. Find f(8) and f(40), and say what each one means.</li><li>For f(s) = 212 &minus; s, the number of hours spent cannot be negative and cannot take the balance below zero. What is the domain, and what is the range?</li><li>Is x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 25 a function of x? Explain using the definition of a function.</li></ol>`,key:["f(8) = 204 and f(40) = 172. f(8) means that after 8 hours are spent the balance is 204 hours, and f(40) means that after 40 hours are spent the balance is 172. (HS F-IF.1 and F-IF.2, function notation)","The domain is every value of s from 0 to 212, that is 0 ≤ s ≤ 212. The range is every balance from 0 to 212, that is 0 ≤ f(s) ≤ 212. Accept whole numbers only if the student says why. (HS F-IF.1, domain and range)","No. A function assigns exactly one output to each input. Here the input x = 3 gives y² = 16, so y can be 4 or −4, which is two outputs for one input. The graph is a circle, and a vertical line at x = 3 crosses it twice. (HS F-IF.1, the definition of a function)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Same Step or Same Ratio",std:["F-LE.1"],needs:["8.F.A.3"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer and how you decided.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The bank's idle balances stand at 1,460 hours and grow by 40 hours a year. Is the total a linear function or an exponential function of the number of years? Write the rule.</li><li>Suppose every person who is helped goes on to help two more people, so after n rounds the number of people helped in that round is 2<sup>n</sup>. Linear or exponential? How do you know?</li><li>This table shows a quantity measured each year. Is it linear or exponential? Write the rule.</li></ol>${bigTable(["Year, t","0","1","2","3"],[["Value, v","64","80","100","125"]])}`,key:["Linear. The same amount, 40 hours, is added over equal intervals, and a linear function is the one with equal differences over equal intervals. The rule is H = 1460 + 40y. (HS F-LE.1a and F-LE.1b)","Exponential. Each round multiplies the number by the same factor, 2, rather than adding the same amount, and a function that changes by a constant ratio over equal intervals is exponential. (HS F-LE.1a and F-LE.1c)","Exponential, with the rule v = 64(1.25)ᵗ. The differences are not constant: 80 − 64 = 16, 100 − 80 = 20, and 125 − 100 = 25. The ratios are constant: 80 ÷ 64 = 1.25, 100 ÷ 80 = 1.25, and 125 ÷ 100 = 1.25. (HS F-LE.1)"]},7:{id:"L7",title:"Level Up: Transformations as Functions",std:["G-CO.2"],needs:["8.G.A.3"],n:3,dir:"Answer out loud. Use coordinates in every answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Write the 90 degree counterclockwise rotation about the origin as a function that takes a point and returns a point, then apply it twice to Q(6, 1). What single transformation do the two rotations together make?</li><li>Of the five transformations on the last stop, which preserve distance and angle, and which do not? Give the rule for one that does not and show what it does to a length.</li><li>Triangle PQR has P(2, 1), Q(6, 1), R(6, 4). A transformation sends P to (4, 2), Q to (12, 2) and R to (12, 8). Name it exactly, and say whether the image is congruent to PQR, similar to it, or neither.</li></ol>`,key:["The rule is (x, y) → (−y, x). Applying it to Q(6, 1) gives (−1, 6), and applying it again gives (−6, −1). Two 90 degree counterclockwise rotations make one 180 degree rotation about the origin, which matches the rule (x, y) → (−x, −y) applied to (6, 1). (HS G-CO.2, transformations as functions and composition)","Translations, reflections, and rotations preserve both distance and angle, so they are rigid motions. The dilation does not preserve distance. Its rule here is (x, y) → (2x, 2y), and it takes PQ from 4 units to 8 units. It does preserve angle, which is why the image is similar rather than congruent. (HS G-CO.2, which transformations preserve distance and angle)","A dilation centered at the origin with a scale factor of 2. The image is similar to PQR but not congruent to it, because the angles match but every length is doubled. (HS G-CO.2)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Angles on the Ladder",std:["G-SRT.8"],needs:["8.G.B.7"],n:2,dir:"Work on your paper with a calculator. Show the ratio you used and hold it up.",mats:["a calculator"],html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A ramp meets the ground at an angle of 8 degrees and its run along the ground is 12 m. How high is the top of the ramp, to the nearest tenth of a meter?</li><li>The 17 ft ladder in Picture 2 has its foot 8 ft from the wall. What angle does it make with the ground, to the nearest degree? Check your answer with the Pythagorean theorem.</li></ol>`,key:["About 1.7 m. The height is opposite the 8 degree angle and the 12 m run is next to it, so tan 8° = height ÷ 12, which gives height = 12 × tan 8° = 12 × 0.14054 = 1.6865, or 1.7 m. (HS G-SRT.8, a trigonometric ratio in an applied problem)","About 62 degrees. The 8 ft run is next to the angle and the 17 ft ladder is the hypotenuse, so cos θ = 8 ÷ 17 = 0.4706 and θ = 61.93°, which rounds to 62°. Check: the Pythagorean theorem gives a height of 15 ft, because 17² − 8² = 225, and sin 61.93° = 15 ÷ 17 = 0.882, which agrees. (HS G-SRT.8, a trigonometric ratio and the Pythagorean theorem together)"]},8:{id:"L8",title:"Level Up: Name the Fallacy",std:["RI.9-10.8"],needs:["RI.8.8"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Quote the sentence you are judging.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Identify one false statement or one piece of fallacious reasoning in the proposal, name the kind of error it is, and explain what evidence would be needed to make the claim sound.</li><li>Take the proposal's strongest reason and say what would have to be true for the cap to actually solve the problem that reason describes. Is the proposal's own evidence enough to show it?</li></ol>${propText()}`,key:["Any one, named and explained. The Brayford sentence is a post hoc error, treating a thing that happened afterwards as a thing that happened because of it; to make it sound you would need the membership trend before the cap, a comparison town that did not cap, and some account of what else changed. The third reason contains a false application: it reports a finding about people who have been helped and applies it to people who have merely been stopped from earning, which are not the same group; to make it sound you would need evidence that capped members go on to accept help rather than simply stop volunteering. The line “Nobody at the bottom asks for help on a page that looks like that” is an unsupported universal, with no evidence offered at all. (HS RI.9-10.8, identifying false statements and fallacious reasoning)","The strongest reason is the hoarding one. For the cap to solve it, the hours above 40 would have to come back into circulation as help actually done, which means either the capped members start spending or the pooled hours get used. The proposal shows that the hours are idle. It shows nothing about what capping would make those eleven people do, and the one piece of counter-evidence in front of the meeting is that 3 of the 11 said they would leave, which would take their future earning out as well. So the reason establishes the problem and not the remedy. (HS RI.9-10.8, evaluating whether the evidence is sufficient)"]},10:{id:"L10",title:"Level Up: Phrase It Together",std:["L.9-10.1"],needs:["L.8.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each new sentence out loud, then name the phrase or clause you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Combine these into one sentence using a participial phrase: “The ledger lay open on the table. It showed fifteen years of entries.”</li><li>Combine these into one sentence using a subordinate clause that begins with Because: “Hours are earned on the bank's own projects. The balances do not add up to zero.”</li><li>Combine these into one sentence using an appositive phrase: “Olu Brandt wrote the entry in ink. She is the member with the largest balance in the book.”</li></ol>`,key:["“Lying open on the table, the ledger showed fifteen years of entries.” Names the participial phrase: Lying open on the table. Accept any correct version built on a participial phrase. (HS L.9-10.1, various types of phrases and clauses)","“Because hours are earned on the bank's own projects, the balances do not add up to zero.” Names it as a subordinate clause. (HS L.9-10.1)","“Olu Brandt, the member with the largest balance in the book, wrote the entry in ink.” Names the appositive phrase: the member with the largest balance in the book. (HS L.9-10.1)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","RI.8.8","RI.8.9","RL.9-10.1","RI.9-10.8"];const WRITE_STD=["W.8.1","W.8.2","W.8.3"];const MATH_STD=["8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.G.A.3","8.G.B.7","8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.SP.A.1","F-IF.1","F-LE.1","G-CO.2","G-SRT.8"];const TEXT_STD=["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","RI.8.8","RI.8.9","W.8.8","RL.9-10.1","RI.9-10.8"];const LOG_NAMES={readq:"Questions read aloud",passage:"Text read aloud by an adult",repeat:"Directions repeated or reworded",vocab:"Word or phrase explained by teacher",hint:"Hint or first step given",fewer:"Fewer questions",time:"Extra time",scribe:"Adult wrote the student's words",brk:"Break"};function stopLogs(st){const out=["readq"];if(READ_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4","W.8.2"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("passage");out.push("repeat","vocab","hint","fewer","time");if(WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("scribe");out.push("brk");return out}const dl=a=>`<dl>${a.map(x=>`<dt>${x[0]}</dt><dd>${x[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const ul=a=>`<ul class="card-list">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function wtOf(codes){const s=codes||[];return s.includes("W.8.8")?"notes":s.includes("SL.8.4")?"say":s.includes("W.8.1")?"argue":s.includes("W.8.3")?"story":"explain"}function wt(st){return wtOf(st.std)}function wtList(st){const ps=st&&st.parts&&st.parts.length?st.parts:[st];const out=[];ps.forEach(p=>{const k=wtOf(p&&p.std&&p.std.length?p.std:st.std);if(!out.includes(k))out.push(k)});return out.length?out:[wt(st)]}const WT_NAME={argue:"Argument",story:"Narrative",explain:"Explaining",notes:"Research notes",say:"Speaking"};function stopSpeak(){try{window.speechSynthesis&&window.speechSynthesis.cancel()}catch(e){}}function guardSpeech(){["mapBtn","teacherBtn","newBtn","prevBtn","gemBtn","skipBtn"].forEach(i=>{const el=document.getElementById(i);if(el&&!el.dataset.tts){el.dataset.tts="1";el.addEventListener("click",stopSpeak)}})}function speakText(t){stopSpeak();guardSpeech();try{const u=new SpeechSynthesisUtterance(t);u.rate=+(document.getElementById("ttsRate")||{value:.9}).value;window.speechSynthesis.speak(u)}catch(e){}}function textOf(sel){return[...document.querySelectorAll(sel)].map(e=>e.textContent.trim()).join(" ")}function listenHtml(){return`<div class="blk-row"><button class="chip" data-say="p">Play the text</button><button class="chip" data-say="q">Play the questions</button><button class="chip" data-say="x">Stop</button></div><label class="small">Speed <select id="ttsRate"><option value="0.75">Slow</option><option value="0.9" selected>Normal</option><option value="1.1">Fast</option></select></label><p class="small">Uses this computer's voice. If you hear nothing, check the volume, then read the text aloud yourself and mark “Text read aloud by an adult.”</p>`}function listenBind(b){b.querySelectorAll("[data-say]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.say,card="#stopView .card ";if(k==="x")return stopSpeak();if(k==="p")speakText(textOf(card+".passage"));if(k==="q")speakText(textOf(card+"ol.qlist > li:not([hidden]), "+card+"> .big"))})}const G_STORY=[["time bank","a group whose members earn an hour of credit for every hour of help they give, and spend those hours on help from somebody else"],["ledger","the book a group keeps its accounts in"],["balance","the number of hours a member holds, which can be below zero"],["deficit","a balance below zero, meaning more help taken than given"],["accountancy","the work of keeping accounts for a business"],["gutters","the channels along the edge of a roof that carry rain away"],["resent","to feel sore about having to do something"]];const G_ART=[["contagious","spreading from one person to another"],["field study","a study run in real places rather than in a laboratory"],["percentage point","the plain difference between two percentages. 26 percent against 15 percent is a gap of 11 percentage points."],["paying it forward","helping somebody new after being helped yourself, rather than paying the helper back"],["obligation","the sense of owing something to somebody"],["durable","lasting a long time"],["financial year","the twelve months a group keeps its accounts over"]];const G_PROP=[["cap","an upper limit a number is not allowed to pass"],["hoarding","keeping far more of something than you use"],["deficit","a balance below zero"],["seed","here, to start something off so that it grows"],["treasurer","the member who looks after a group's money or accounts"],["community pool","a shared store that any member can draw on"]];const G_SRC=[["credible","able to be trusted"],["plagiarism","using someone else's words or ideas as if they were your own"],["paraphrase","to say it in your own words"],["citation","the line that says where a fact came from"],["replication","running a study again, somewhere else, to see whether the result holds"],["detectable","big enough to be measured"]];const G_MATH=[["function","a rule that gives each input exactly one output"],["input and output","what goes into a rule and what comes out of it"],["linear function","one whose graph is a straight line, written y = mx + b"],["slope","how much y changes for each 1 that x changes"],["translation","a slide, with no turning and no resizing"],["reflection","a flip across a line"],["rotation","a turn about a point"],["dilation","a resize from a center point, which keeps the shape and changes the size"],["congruent","the same shape and the same size"],["similar","the same shape at a different size"],["hypotenuse","the longest side of a right triangle, across from the right angle"]];const G_WRITE={explain:[["informative writing","writing that explains rather than argues"],["objective tone","sticking to what is known, with no side taken and no exclamations"],["formal style","writing with no slang, no texting language, and no jokes to the reader"],["transition","a word or phrase that shows how one part relates to the next"],["concluding statement","a last sentence that follows from what came before"]],notes:[["search terms","the words you type to find sources"],["source","where a fact came from"],["quotation marks","the marks that show you are using somebody's exact words"]],say:[["recommendation","what you are telling the meeting to do"],["drawback","the cost or weakness of your own choice"],["ruled out","shown not to be the cause"]]};function glossHtml(st){if(st.id===11)return dl(G_WRITE.explain.concat(G_ART,G_PROP));if(st.id===12)return dl(G_WRITE.notes.concat(G_SRC,G_ART));if(st.id===13)return dl(G_WRITE.say.concat(G_PROP));if(st.id===4)return dl(G_ART.concat(G_STORY));if(st.id===8)return dl(G_PROP.concat(G_ART));if(st.std.some(x=>MATH_STD.includes(x)))return dl(G_MATH);return dl(G_STORY)}const TERMS=[["cite","point to the exact words in the text that prove your answer"],["strongest evidence","the words that prove the answer best, not just words on the same topic"],["explicit","stated right in the text"],["inference","something the text does not say outright, but that you can figure out from clues"],["connotation","the feeling a word carries beyond its plain meaning"],["figurative language","words that mean something other than what they literally say"],["dialogue","the words characters say out loud"],["incident","one event in a story"],["propel the action","push the story forward so that something new has to happen"],["motivation","what a character wants, and why"],["claim","what a writer wants you to believe or do"],["relevant","actually about the claim"],["sufficient","enough to prove the point"],["sound reasoning","thinking that makes sense and follows from the evidence"],["post hoc","treating something that happened afterwards as something that happened because of it"],["thorough evidence","enough details, from more than one place in the text"]];const FRAMES={cite:["The answer is ____. The words that prove it are “____.”","The strongest evidence is “____” because ____.","I can infer that ____ because the text says “____.”"],"RL.8.3":["When ____ says “____,” it makes ____ decide to ____.","Because ____ happened, ____ now has to ____.","This line shows that ____ is ____ because ____."],"RL.8.4":["The word ____ suggests ____, while ____ suggests ____.","This is ____ language, and it makes the reader see ____.","The author chose ____ instead of ____, which has the effect of ____."],"RI.8.8":["Vance claims that ____.","One reason he gives is ____, backed by ____.","That evidence is relevant because ____ and sufficient because ____.","The sentence about ____ does not belong because ____.","This reasoning is weak because ____."],"RI.9-10.8":["The sentence “____” is unsound because ____.","That is a ____ error.","To make it sound you would need ____."]};function framesHtml(st){const k=st.std.includes("RI.9-10.8")?"RI.9-10.8":st.std.includes("RI.8.8")?"RI.8.8":st.std.includes("RL.8.4")?"RL.8.4":st.std.includes("RL.8.3")?"RL.8.3":"cite";return`<ul class="starters" style="font-size:1.2rem">${FRAMES[k].map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function gramHtml(){return`<h4>Verbals: verb forms doing another job</h4>`+dl([["Gerund","ends in -ing and acts as a noun. <em>Copying the balances is slow work.</em>"],["Participle","often ends in -ing or -ed and describes a noun. <em>The waiting member signed in.</em>"],["Infinitive","to plus a verb. <em>She hoped to finish by noon.</em>"]])+`<h4>Voice</h4>`+dl([["Active","the subject does the action. <em>A member did the gutters.</em>"],["Passive","the subject receives the action. <em>The gutters were done by a member.</em>"]])+`<h4>Mood</h4>`+dl([["Indicative","states a fact. <em>The ledger is in the drawer.</em>"],["Imperative","gives a command. <em>Check the column.</em>"],["Interrogative","asks a question. <em>Is the column checked?</em>"],["Conditional","tells what would or could happen. <em>I would spend them if they were mine.</em>"],["Subjunctive","a wish or something not true. Uses were. <em>If the ledger were easier to read, more people would use it.</em>"]])+`<h4>Phrases and clauses</h4>`+dl([["Participial phrase","a participle plus its words, describing a noun. <em>Lying open on the table, the ledger showed its entries.</em>"],["Subordinate clause","has a subject and a verb but cannot stand alone. <em>Because hours are earned on projects, the balances do not sum to zero.</em>"],["Appositive phrase","renames the noun right beside it. <em>Olu Brandt, the member with the largest balance, wrote it in ink.</em>"]])+`<p class="small">Keep the same voice and the same mood all the way through a sentence.</p>`}const PLAN={explain:["What the time bank is, in one sentence","The rule: how an hour is earned and spent","The numbers: size and pattern","The argument: both proposals, fairly","What is not known"],notes:["Which source I trust, and why","My search terms","My quote or paraphrase","My citation"],say:["My recommendation","My strongest evidence from the table","The drawback","What I need from the meeting"],argue:["My claim","My evidence, with the number","The strongest point on the other side","Why my claim still holds"],story:["Where I am and what I know","What happens first","What is said or thought","How the moment ends"]};function planHtml(st){const S=E.state;S.plan=S.plan||{};const k=wt(st),v=S.plan[k]||[];return`<div class="planner">${PLAN[k].map((x,i)=>`<label>${x}<textarea data-pi="${i}">${(v[i]||"").replace(/</g,"&lt;")}</textarea></label>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Your plan is saved for the speaking stop and the writing stop.</p>`}function planBind(b,r,st){b.querySelectorAll("[data-pi]").forEach(t=>t.oninput=()=>{const S=E.state,k=wt(st);S.plan=S.plan||{};S.plan[k]=S.plan[k]||[];S.plan[k][+t.dataset.pi]=t.value;E.save()})}const SENT={explain:{s:["The Westmill Time Bank is ____.","The rule is that ____.","Last year ____.","The argument is about ____.","One proposal would ____. The other would ____.","What the ledger cannot show is ____."],w:["first","in addition","for example","as a result","however","by contrast","in short"]},argue:{s:["The bank should ____.","The evidence is ____.","Someone would say ____.","Even so, ____."],w:["because","however","even so","the ledger shows","for that reason"]},story:{s:["I was ____ when ____.","I knew ____.","“____,” ____ said.","By the time ____, ____."],w:["then","after that","by the time","at last"]},notes:{s:["I trust Source ____ more because ____.","These search terms are best because ____.","Dr. Priya Ramaswamy reports, “____.”","According to Dr. Priya Ramaswamy, ____.","Author. “Title.” Publisher, year."],w:["according to","reports","states","explains"]},say:{s:["The meeting should ____.","My strongest evidence is ____.","The drawback is ____.","For that reason, ____.","From the members I need ____."],w:["because","the table shows","compared with","by contrast","for that reason"]}};function sentHtml(st){const ks=wtList(st).filter(k=>SENT[k]),many=ks.length>1;return(ks.length?ks:["explain"]).map(k=>{const d=SENT[k]||SENT.explain;return(many?`<h4>${WT_NAME[k]}</h4>`:"")+`<ul class="starters">${d.s.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul><h4>Linking words</h4><div class="wordbank">${d.w.map(x=>`<span>${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`}).join("")}const CHECK={argue:["I stated my claim in the first sentence.","My evidence is from the texts and the number is right.","I named the strongest point on the other side, not the weakest.","I answered it.","I reread it for mistakes."],story:["I set up where I am and what I know.","There is a line of dialogue or a thought.","The events are in an order that makes sense.","The ending follows from the moment.","I reread it for mistakes."],explain:["I introduced the topic and said what was coming.","Related information is grouped, not mixed together.","I used facts and numbers from the fact box, accurately.","I explained both proposals without taking a side.","I used transitions between the parts.","I used the words of the topic: credit, balance, deficit, cap.","I kept a formal, objective tone.","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."]};function checkHtml(st){const ks=wtList(st).filter(k=>CHECK[k]),many=ks.length>1;return(ks.length?ks:["explain"]).map(k=>(many?`<h4>${WT_NAME[k]}</h4>`:"")+`<ul class="card-list" style="list-style:none;padding-left:0">${(CHECK[k]||CHECK.explain).map(x=>`<li><label style="display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:flex-start"><input type="checkbox" style="width:20px;height:20px;margin-top:4px"> ${x}</label></li>`).join("")}</ul>`).join("")}let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(){let e=calcE.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-").replace(/\^/g,"**").replace(/√\(/g,"Math.sqrt(");if(!/^[0-9+\-*/().\sMathsqr]*$/.test(e)||!e)return"";try{const o=(e.match(/\(/g)||[]).length-(e.match(/\)/g)||[]).length;if(o>0)e+=")".repeat(o);const v=Function('"use strict";return ('+e+")")();return Number.isFinite(v)?String(+v.toPrecision(10)):"Error"}catch(x){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","(","1","2","3","−",")","0",".","^","+","C","√(","x²","="];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${esc(calcE)||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${esc(calcAns)}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k==="^"?"xʸ":k==="√("?"√":k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Press √ then the number and close the bracket. For a square, press x². This calculator has no sine, cosine, or tangent key.</p>`}function calcBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.replace(/(√\(|.)$/,"");calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval()}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷^x]/.test(k))calcE=calcAns;else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k==="x²"?"^2":k}r()})}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function sqHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>n</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=25;n++)h+=`<th>${n}</th>`;h+=`</tr><tr><th>n²</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=25;n++)h+=`<td>${n*n}</td>`;return h+`</tr></table></div><p class="small">n² means n × n. To find a square root, find the number in the n² row and look up to n. 225 is in the row, so the square root of 225 is 15.</p>`}const rightTri=`<svg viewBox="0 0 220 130" width="220" role="img" aria-label="Right triangle with legs a and b and hypotenuse c" style="display:block;margin:0 auto"><polygon points="20,110 190,110 20,15" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="20" y="96" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="105" y="128" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">b</text><text x="8" y="68" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">a</text><text x="116" y="54" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">c</text></svg>`;const rules=rows=>`<table class="meas">${rows.map(r=>`<tr><th>${r[0]}</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">${r[1]}</td></tr>`).join("")}</table>`;const FORM={"8.F.A.1":["Functions",ul(["A function is a rule that gives each input exactly one output.","Two different inputs may share an output. One input may not have two different outputs.","The graph of a function is the set of ordered pairs (input, output).","Vertical line test: a vertical line touches the graph of a function at most once."])],"8.F.A.3":["Linear functions",`<p class="formula">y = mx + b</p>`+ul(["m is the slope, the change in y for each 1 of x. b is the value when x is 0.","The graph is a straight line, and the change is the same over every equal step.","If the rule squares x, or puts x on the bottom of a fraction, or uses x as an exponent, it is not linear.","To test a table: equal steps in x should give equal changes in y."])],"8.G.A.3":["Transformations on the coordinate plane",rules([["Translation","(x, y) → (x + a, y + b)"],["Reflection over the y-axis","(x, y) → (−x, y)"],["Reflection over the x-axis","(x, y) → (x, −y)"],["Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin","(x, y) → (−y, x)"],["Rotation 180° about the origin","(x, y) → (−x, −y)"],["Dilation from the origin, factor k","(x, y) → (kx, ky)"]])+ul(["Translations, reflections, and rotations keep every length and every angle, so the image is congruent.","A dilation keeps every angle and multiplies every length by k, so the image is similar.","A dilation by k multiplies the area by k²."])],"8.G.B.7":["Pythagorean Theorem",rightTri+`<p class="formula">a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup></p>`+ul(["a and b are the legs. c is the hypotenuse, the longest side, across from the right angle.","To find a leg instead: c² − a² = b².","On a grid, the legs are the horizontal and vertical distances between the two points.","For a box: length² + width² + height² = diagonal²."])],"8.NS.A.1":["Rational and irrational numbers",ul(["A rational number can be written as one whole number over another. Its decimal either stops or repeats a block forever.","A decimal that never ends and never repeats is irrational. π and √2 are irrational.","To turn a repeating decimal into a fraction: call it x, multiply by 10 for each repeating digit, subtract, then solve."])],"8.EE.A.1":["Integer exponents",`<p class="formula">a<sup>m</sup> &times; a<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>m+n</sup> &nbsp; a<sup>m</sup> &divide; a<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>m&minus;n</sup> &nbsp; (a<sup>m</sup>)<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>mn</sup></p>`+ul(["a to the 0 is 1 for any non-zero a.","a to the negative n is 1 over a to the n.","Only combine powers that have the same base."])],"8.EE.A.2":["Square and cube roots",ul(["√ asks what number squared gives this. ∛ asks what number cubed gives this.","x² = p has two solutions, the positive root and the negative one. x³ = p has one.","Perfect squares to know: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100. Perfect cubes: 1, 8, 27, 64, 125.","√2 is irrational: it cannot be written as one whole number over another."])],"8.EE.B.5":["Slope as a unit rate",ul(["In a proportional relationship y = mx, m is both the unit rate and the slope of the line.","From a table: divide y by x. Every pair must give the same answer.","To compare two relationships given in different forms, put both into the same form first.","A proportional line passes through (0, 0)."])],"8.EE.C.8":["Two equations at once",ul(["The solution is the point where the two lines cross, so it satisfies both equations.","Substitution: make one equation say x = something, then put that into the other.","Elimination: add or subtract the equations so one letter disappears.","Parallel lines: no solution. The same line twice: infinitely many."])],"8.SP.A.1":["Scatter plots",ul(["Positive association: as one goes up, so does the other. Negative: as one goes up, the other goes down.","Linear association: the points lie close to one straight line.","An outlier sits well away from the pattern the rest of the points make.","Clustering: a group of points bunched together in one part of the plot."])],"F-LE.1":["Linear or exponential",ul(["<strong>Linear:</strong> equal differences over equal intervals. The same amount is added or taken away each step.","<strong>Exponential:</strong> equal ratios over equal intervals. The value is multiplied by the same factor each step.","To test a table: find the differences first, then the ratios. Whichever is constant names the kind."])],"F-IF.1":["Function notation",ul(["f(x) is the output of the rule f when the input is x. f(8) means put 8 in.","The domain is the set of allowed inputs. The range is the set of outputs they produce.","f(8) = 204 is one ordered pair of the function, (8, 204)."])],"G-CO.2":["Transformations as functions",ul(["A transformation takes a point and returns a point, so it can be written as a rule on (x, y).","Rigid motions, translations, reflections, and rotations, preserve distance and angle.","A dilation preserves angle but not distance.","Doing one transformation and then another is a composition. Two 90° turns make one 180° turn."])],"G-SRT.8":["Right triangle trigonometry",rules([["Sine","sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse"],["Cosine","cos θ = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse"],["Tangent","tan θ = opposite ÷ adjacent"]])+ul(["Label the sides from the angle you are working with first.","To find an angle from a ratio, use the inverse key on a calculator.","The Pythagorean theorem still holds and is the quickest check."])]};function formHtml(st){const seen=[];return st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>{if(seen.includes(FORM[x][0]))return"";seen.push(FORM[x][0]);return`<h4>${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`}).join("")}const EX={"8.G.A.3":[["Apply a transformation rule to every vertex",["Triangle with A(1, 2), B(4, 2), C(4, 6).","Rule: reflect over the y-axis, so (x, y) → (−x, y).","A(1, 2) → (−1, 2). B(4, 2) → (−4, 2). C(4, 6) → (−4, 6).","Check one length: AB was 3 and the image is 3, because a reflection keeps lengths."]],["Dilate from the origin",["Same triangle, scale factor 3, so (x, y) → (3x, 3y).","A → (3, 6). B → (12, 6). C → (12, 18).","AB goes from 3 to 9, which is 3 times. The area goes up by 3², which is 9 times."]]],"8.F.A.3":[["Decide whether a table is linear",["Table: x = 0, 1, 2, 3 and y = 5, 9, 13, 17.","x goes up by 1 each step, so check the changes in y: 4, 4, 4.","The changes are equal, so it is linear.","b is the value at x = 0, which is 5, and m is the change, which is 4, so y = 4x + 5."]]],"8.G.B.7":[["Find a missing side",["Legs are 9 and 12. Find the hypotenuse c.","9² + 12² = c²","81 + 144 = 225","c is the square root of 225, which is 15.","To find a leg instead, subtract: c² − a² = b²."]]]};function exHtml(st){const seen=[];return st.std.filter(x=>EX[x]).map(x=>EX[x].map(e=>{if(seen.includes(e[0]))return"";seen.push(e[0]);return`<h4>${e[0]}</h4><ol class="card-list">${e[1].map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join("")}</ol>`}).join("")).join("")+`<p class="small">These are different problems from the ones on the stop. Use the same steps.</p>`}let gridPts=[],gridJoin=false;const GS=440,GM=10,GU=(GS-68)/(2*GM);function pgridHtml(){const lines=[];if(gridJoin)for(let i=1;i<gridPts.length;i++)lines.push([gridPts[i-1],gridPts[i]]);return`<div class="blk-row"><button class="chip" data-g="join" aria-pressed="${gridJoin}">Connect the points</button><button class="chip" data-g="undo">Undo</button><button class="chip" data-g="clear">Clear</button></div><div class="figure" data-pg style="cursor:crosshair">${F.coordGrid({max:GM,quad4:true,size:GS,points:gridPts,lines})}</div><p class="small">Click the grid to plot a point. Points: ${gridPts.map(p=>`(${p[0]}, ${p[1]})`).join(", ")||"none yet"}</p>`}function pgridBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-g]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const g=x.dataset.g;if(g==="join")gridJoin=!gridJoin;if(g==="undo")gridPts.pop();if(g==="clear")gridPts=[];r()});const el=b.querySelector("[data-pg] svg");if(!el)return;el.onclick=e=>{const q=el.getBoundingClientRect(),vx=(e.clientX-q.left)*GS/q.width,vy=(e.clientY-q.top)*GS/q.height,x=Math.round((vx-34)/GU)-GM,y=Math.round((GS-34-vy)/GU)-GM;if(Math.abs(x)>GM||Math.abs(y)>GM)return;gridPts.push([x,y]);r()}}const TOOLS={listen:{name:"Computer reads it aloud",std:TEXT_STD.concat(["W.8.2"]),ok:()=>"speechSynthesis"in window,html:listenHtml,bind:listenBind},gloss:{name:"Word help",std:TEXT_STD.concat(WRITE_STD,MATH_STD,["SL.8.4"]),html:glossHtml},terms:{name:"Reading terms",std:READ_STD,html:()=>dl(TERMS)},frames:{name:"Answer frames",std:READ_STD,html:framesHtml},gram:{name:"Grammar card",std:["L.8.1","L.9-10.1"],html:gramHtml},plan:{name:"Planner",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]),html:planHtml,bind:planBind},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]),html:sentHtml},check:{name:"Writing checklist",std:WRITE_STD,html:checkHtml},calc:{name:"Calculator",std:MATH_STD,html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})},sq:{name:"Squares chart",std:["8.G.B.7","G-SRT.8"],html:sqHtml},form:{name:"Formula and definition card",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},ex:{name:"Worked example",std:Object.keys(EX),html:exHtml},grid:{name:"Coordinate grid",std:["8.G.A.3","8.G.B.7","8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.SP.A.1","G-CO.2","F-IF.1"],html:pgridHtml,bind:pgridBind}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"listen",std:READ_STD,msg:"If the computer read the text aloud, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"passage",msg:"If the text, the fact box, or the table was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"terms",std:READ_STD,msg:"The reading terms card defines cite, inference, connotation, relevant, sufficient, and post hoc, so this stop then shows applying those ideas with a reference."},{t:"frames",msg:"Answer frames give the sentence structure. Rate the evidence and the thinking the student supplied."},{t:"frames",std:["RI.8.8","RI.9-10.8"],msg:"The frames for this stop name the parts of an argument and the shape of a fallacy answer, so count the sorting part of the answer as supported and rate the evidence the student found."},{t:"gram",std:["L.8.1","L.9-10.1"],msg:"The grammar card defines verbals, voice, mood, and phrase types with examples, so this stop shows using a reference, not recall."},{t:"plan",msg:"The planner supplies the organization, so count the organization check as supported, not independent."},{t:"plan",std:["W.8.2"],msg:"The planner names the parts the explanation has to cover, including both proposals, so count the grouping check and the fairness check as supported."},{t:"sent",msg:"Sentence frames supply the linking words and the closing, so count those checks as supported."},{t:"check",msg:"The checklist names what to include. Note that the student used it to revise."},{t:"calc",std:["8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","F-IF.1","F-LE.1"],msg:"The calculator worked out the values. Rate whether the student knew what to compute and what the result shows."},{t:"calc",std:["8.G.B.7","G-SRT.8"],msg:"The calculator has a square root key, so rate whether the student knew which squares to add or subtract, not the root itself."},{t:"calc",std:["8.G.A.3","G-CO.2"],msg:"The calculator does not help with a transformation rule. Note only that it was open."},{t:"sq",std:["8.G.B.7","G-SRT.8"],msg:"The squares chart gives the square roots, so count the final step of each question as supported and rate the setup."},{t:"form",std:["8.F.A.1"],msg:"The card states the definition of a function and the vertical line test, so this stop then shows applying a stated rule rather than recalling it."},{t:"form",std:["8.F.A.3","F-LE.1"],msg:"The card says how to test a table and which forms are not linear, so count the sorting questions as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.G.A.3","G-CO.2"],msg:"The card lists the coordinate rule for every transformation on this stop and states what each one preserves, so this stop shows applying a stated rule rather than working it out. Note it on the record sheet."},{t:"form",std:["8.G.B.7","G-SRT.8"],msg:"The card gives the theorem, the rule for a leg, the three-dimensional version, and the trigonometric ratios, so this stop shows applying them with a reference."},{t:"form",std:["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2"],msg:"The card gives the exponent rules, the perfect squares and cubes, and the method for turning a repeating decimal into a fraction, so count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.SP.A.1"],msg:"The card gives the unit rate test, both methods for a pair of equations, and the words for describing a scatter plot, so this stop then shows applying a stated method rather than recalling it."},{t:"calc",std:["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8"],msg:"The calculator has a power key and a root key. Rate whether the student chose the right rule or the right equation, not the arithmetic."},{t:"grid",std:["8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.SP.A.1"],msg:"If the student plotted the points or the two lines on the grid tool, note that the slope, the crossing point, or the trend was read off a picture rather than worked out."},{t:"ex",msg:"A worked example models the method. Count the matching questions as supported."},{t:"grid",std:["8.G.A.3","G-CO.2"],msg:"If the student plotted the image points on the grid tool instead of working them out from the rule, note that the answer came from a picture rather than from the coordinates."},{t:"grid",std:["8.G.B.7"],msg:"If the grid tool was used to count the horizontal and vertical legs, note that the student read them off a picture rather than subtracting the coordinates."},{t:"scribe",msg:"If an adult wrote, rate the ideas and the organization. Do not rate conventions the adult supplied."},{t:"fewer",msg:"If questions were skipped, change the “of” number in the score to the number the student tried."},{t:"hint",msg:"Note which question got the hint, and count that question as supported."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,logNames:LOG_NAMES,stopLogs,onToolChange:()=>stopSpeak()}}});
